window.COURSE = {"title": "Quantum Machine Learning", "preface": {"id": "preface", "title": "About This Book", "body_file": "pages/preface.html", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">What would change in a machine-learning model if part of its computation were performed by a quantum circuit? How would we represent the data, train the model, and decide whether the quantum component helped? These are the questions that motivate this book.</p>\n<section id=\"purpose\"><h2>Why quantum machine learning?</h2>\n<p>Quantum machine learning brings ideas from quantum computing into the study and design of learning methods. A model may encode data into quantum states, use a parameterized circuit to transform those states, and form a prediction from measurements. Those choices raise familiar learning questions in a new computational setting.</p>\n<p>The presence of a quantum circuit does not settle whether a model is useful. We still need a clear task, suitable data, a training procedure and a fair comparison. Our aim is to understand the complete process well enough to build small experiments and evaluate what they show.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/qml-learning-loop.svg\" alt=\"Classical data are encoded into a state, a parameterized quantum circuit is measured, and a classical loss and optimizer update its parameters.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure P1.</strong> A quantum component inside a learning process. A parameter update can require many circuit evaluations. This is a model of the workflow, not evidence of a performance advantage.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Keep this learning loop in view as we develop the foundations. A state representation will become an encoding choice. A gate parameter will become something we can train. A measurement will become part of a prediction. The mathematics has a purpose in the model we eventually want to understand.</p></section>\n<section id=\"audience\"><h2>Who is this book for?</h2>\n<p>These notes are for quantum-computing and machine-learning enthusiasts, students, software developers, and professionals who want to understand QML and begin experimenting with it. If you already work with machine learning, you will have familiar ideas to build upon. If you are exploring the subject for the first time, the examples and prerequisite explanations provide a route into it.</p>\n<p>A prior course in quantum physics is not required. We approach the subject through information, computation and learning. Basic algebra and elementary probability will help you begin. We introduce matrix, complex-number and further mathematical tools near their use. Familiarity with Python and basic ML will help with the programming and later chapters.</p>\n<p>My aim is to help you move from curiosity to understanding: to explain a quantum learning model, reproduce a small experiment, and ask informed questions about its usefulness and limitations. The emphasis is on a careful foundation for exploration and professional judgment.</p></section>\n<section id=\"foundations\"><h2>Why begin with quantum computing?</h2>\n<p>Our destination is quantum machine learning. To reach it, we need to understand the tools from which quantum learning models are built. Before constructing a quantum classifier, we need to know what a state represents, how a circuit changes it and what measurement allows us to observe.</p>\n<p>A software developer can work with a computer through a computational model without first designing its transistors. We will use a similar idea of abstraction. The relevant physical principles will be explained, while the state, circuit and measurement descriptions give us a precise language for reasoning.</p>\n<p>We will not skip the mathematics that makes these descriptions meaningful. Definitions, derivations and worked examples will show how each result follows. Familiar analogies help us enter a topic; we will also explain where those analogies stop being sufficient.</p></section>\n<section id=\"organization\"><h2>The three-part route</h2>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/book-roadmap.svg\" alt=\"Part A quantum computing foundations, Part B classical machine learning bridge, Part C quantum machine learning and evaluation.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure P2.</strong> The destination is QML. Each part supplies concepts used later, with theory and experiments throughout.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Part A — Quantum Computing Foundations.</strong> We begin with a conceptual introduction, then build the language of states, operations, measurement and circuits. Selected algorithms show how quantum features enter a computation.</p>\n<p><strong>Part B — The Classical Machine Learning Bridge.</strong> We revisit learning, optimization, feature representations, kernels and evaluation. This connects the quantum foundations with the learning methods we want to build.</p>\n<p><strong>Part C — Quantum Machine Learning.</strong> We bring the pieces together through encoding, parameterized circuits, quantum classifiers and quantum kernels, followed by training and careful evaluation against suitable alternatives.</p>\n<p>The Preface and Chapters 1–3 are available. The course outline identifies later material as planned; the roadmap describes the intended complete book.</p></section>\n<section id=\"study\"><h2>How to learn with these notes</h2>\n<p>The written explanations and worked reasoning form the main path. When a question appears, pause and try the next step before revealing its answer. Interactive visualizations let you change a setting and examine its consequences. Quizzes explain the reasoning behind each choice, and exercises encourage you to work independently.</p>\n<p>Academic figures sit beside the concepts they explain. You can pan a wide figure on a narrow screen. Read each figure together with its caption and the surrounding explanation.</p>\n<p>Where programming helps, Python and Qiskit notebooks connect the theory to experiments. Colab links let you open the available companions; quantum-circuit programming will enter after the necessary concepts. Predict a result, calculate what you can by hand, run the experiment and explain what you observe.</p>\n<p>Throughout the book we will return to the original question: how does the quantum component contribute to learning, what does the evidence show, and when is that contribution useful?</p>\n<p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#quantum-introduction\">Begin Part A · Chapter 1</a> <a href=\"#classical-information\">Go to Chapter 2: classical foundations</a></p></section>\n"}, "chapters": [{"id": "quantum-introduction", "title": "Introduction to Quantum Computing", "number": 1, "part": "Part A · Quantum Computing Foundations", "body_file": "chapters/quantum-introduction/overview.html", "lessons": [], "quizzes": [{"id": "intro-phase", "question": "The pure states plus and minus have the same Z probabilities. What does the final Hadamard do in the worked example?", "options": ["It turns their relative-sign difference into different final measurement probabilities.", "It measures both intermediate coefficients directly in one shot.", "It makes every possible input state deterministic in Z."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. H adds and subtracts amplitudes; plus becomes 0 and minus becomes 1 before the final measurement.", "H is a unitary gate, not a measurement. One Z readout records one outcome.", "That conclusion holds for these two particular inputs, not for arbitrary states."]}, {"id": "intro-correlation", "question": "A Bell state and a classical mixture of 00 and 11 give matching Z outcomes. Does that one observation establish entanglement?", "options": ["Yes: every correlation is entanglement.", "No: this classical preparation has the same Z statistics; other measurement choices distinguish the examples.", "Yes: it proves a faster-than-light message was transmitted."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Classical random preparations can be correlated too. The full state and allowed measurements matter.", "Correct. In this example, matching X outcomes occur with probability 1 for the Bell state and 1/2 for the mixture.", "Correlations do not let a party choose its random local outcome to transmit a message."]}, {"id": "intro-purpose", "question": "A proposed quantum classifier runs correctly on a simulator. What can we conclude?", "options": ["It must train faster than every classical model.", "It proves practical quantum advantage.", "It is a candidate experiment whose learning quality and complete resource costs still need evaluation."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["Correct circuit execution alone does not establish a speed comparison.", "Simulation demonstrates the modeled behavior, not an end-to-end practical advantage.", "Correct. Compare a defined learning task with suitable classical baselines and account for preparation, training and readout."]}, {"id": "intro-chsh", "question": "Why can shared classical randomness not beat 75% in the stated CHSH game?", "options": ["It mixes deterministic strategies, each of which wins at most three of the four uniform input cases.", "It lets Alice learn Bob’s input.", "Randomness always lowers every strategy’s score."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. A weighted average cannot exceed the largest score of its components.", "The rules forbid communication during a round; shared randomness does not reveal the private input.", "Some randomized strategies tie the best deterministic score. The upper bound follows from averaging, not from randomness being harmful."]}, {"id": "intro-evaluation", "question": "A quantum circuit uses fewer oracle calls. What else is needed to establish a practical runtime benefit?", "options": ["Nothing: fewer oracle calls settle the complete runtime.", "Account for implementation, preparation, repetitions and processing against a suitable classical baseline.", "Only the number of physical qubits."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["An oracle call can be expensive, and other costs can dominate.", "Correct. The claim must match the complete task, accuracy and costs being compared.", "Qubit count alone does not specify usable computation, reliability or workflow cost."]}], "notebook": "01-chsh-game.ipynb", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Our goal is quantum machine learning. Before we can decide how to use a quantum circuit in a model, we need to know what kind of computation it performs. This chapter gives a first conceptual and mathematical view, with examples we will develop more fully in the foundations that follow.</p>\n<section id=\"applications\"><h2>1. Why quantum computing matters</h2>\n<p>Before asking how a quantum computer works, ask what we would want it to do. We already have powerful classical computers. A new computing model matters if it helps answer a well-defined question with resources that we can justify. The interesting question is not whether the machine is quantum, but what becomes possible when we can control quantum states.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/applications.png\" alt=\"Molecule, crystal lattice, routing graph and lock illustrate four potential application areas.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.1.</strong> Potential applications. The illustrations identify research areas, not established performance guarantees.<br><span class=\"image-source\">Source: Original illustration generated with AI assistance.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Molecules and materials</h3><p>Molecules and materials are quantum systems. Their properties depend on interactions among electrons and nuclei. Describing a general many-particle quantum state on a classical computer can require a very large amount of memory. This motivates using a controllable quantum system to study another quantum system. An algorithm might estimate an energy or a dynamical property, rather than print every amplitude of the state.</p>\n<p>Possible scientific uses include studying chemical reactions, catalysts and material properties. A useful calculation still needs a suitable physical model, an accurate state preparation, a specified observable and enough reliable operations. This motivation does not mean that every chemistry problem is classically intractable, or that a quantum processor automatically produces a new drug.</p>\n<h3>Search, optimization and learning</h3><p>Search and optimization ask us to identify a useful answer among alternatives. Quantum interference can help in particular algorithmic settings. For example, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9605043\">Grover’s search algorithm</a> uses order \\(\\sqrt{N}\\) oracle queries for an unstructured search with one marked item, compared with order \\(N\\) classical queries. That is a query-model result: implementing the oracle, loading inputs and obtaining the answer also cost resources.</p>\n<p>Scheduling, routing and portfolio selection motivate optimization research, but there is no general guarantee that quantum methods outperform strong classical methods on these tasks. QML raises the same careful question for learning: can a quantum representation or transformation help on a specified dataset, with the complete cost included?</p>\n<h3>Cryptography and communication</h3><p><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9508027\">Shor’s algorithms</a> give polynomial-time quantum methods for factoring and discrete logarithms. A sufficiently capable fault-tolerant machine would therefore threaten public-key systems based on these problems, including RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography. This is not a claim that all encryption is broken by any quantum device.</p>\n<p>Quantum communication is a related field. Entanglement and measurement can be used in protocols such as quantum key distribution; secure implementations also need authentication, classical communication and explicit security assumptions. We will separate the computational task from the communication protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway.</strong> Applications supply the motivation. The algorithm, assumptions, accuracy and total resources determine whether a proposed benefit is meaningful.</p></section>\n<section id=\"history\"><h2>2. How quantum mechanics emerged</h2>\n<p>The period around 1900–1930 transformed our description of nature. Classical models remained useful, but could not explain all the observations involving light and matter. Quantum theory developed through a sequence of new ideas, experiments and mathematical formulations, rather than through a single invention.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/solvay-1927.jpg\" alt=\"Group photograph of scientists at the Fifth Solvay Conference in 1927.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.2.</strong> The 1927 Solvay Conference, during a formative period in quantum mechanics.<br><span class=\"image-source\">Source: Benjamin Couprie, Institut International de Physique Solvay; <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solvay_conference_1927.jpg\">Wikimedia Commons image record</a> (public domain).</span></figcaption></figure>\n<ul><li><strong>1900 — Planck:</strong> quantized energy exchange in a model of thermal radiation.</li><li><strong>1905 — Einstein:</strong> light quanta and the photoelectric effect.</li><li><strong>1913 — Bohr:</strong> discrete atomic energy levels in an early atomic model.</li><li><strong>1925–1926:</strong> matrix and wave formulations established the language of modern quantum mechanics.</li><li><strong>1927:</strong> the uncertainty principle and debates about the interpretation of the new theory.</li></ul>\n<p>The Solvay photograph puts people behind this intellectual change: Einstein, Bohr, Curie, Planck and other leading scientists appear together. The <a href=\"https://solvayinstitutes.be/home/archives/conferences-archives/\">Solvay Institutes’ archive</a> records the 1927 meeting on electrons and photons. The photograph gives historical context; the scientific case rests on mathematical predictions and experimental evidence.</p>\n<p>For a short historical route into these discoveries, see the <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1918/summary/\">Planck</a>, <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1921/summary/\">Einstein</a> and <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1922/summary/\">Bohr</a> Nobel Prize records. Our purpose is to understand enough of this language to use quantum computation; a full physics course is not a prerequisite.</p></section>\n<section id=\"motivation\"><h2>3. Keep the learning problem in view</h2>\n<p>Suppose we already have a classical learning pipeline: prepare data, choose a model, fit its parameters and evaluate predictions on held-out examples. Where could a quantum computation enter? It might provide a feature representation, a trainable transformation, or an estimate used in a kernel method. The learning task remains the reason for introducing that component.</p>\n<p>We therefore need two kinds of understanding. We must know the behavior of the quantum computation, and we must know how its input, output and cost connect to the learning problem. A circuit that runs successfully is one part of an experiment; it is not by itself evidence that the resulting model learns better or runs faster.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#preface/purpose\">learning-loop figure in the Preface</a> is our starting map. Classical data and targets enter a larger process that may include quantum state preparation, circuit execution and measurement. A classical optimizer can use the resulting loss estimates to update circuit parameters. Several circuit evaluations may be needed for one update.</p>\n<div data-opening-lab=\"workflow\"></div>\n<p>By the end of this chapter, you should be able to name the stages of this process, explain why measurement limits what we read out, and identify a few questions that must be answered before calling a quantum learning experiment useful.</p></section>\n<section id=\"abstraction\"><h2>4. From understanding nature to harnessing it</h2>\n<p>Quantum mechanics supplies a mathematical description of physical systems. Quantum computing uses appropriate physical behavior to implement computations. The subjects are closely connected, but our immediate learning task is to understand the computational model and the assumptions under which it operates.</p>\n<h3>The agriculture analogy</h3><p>Imagine first observing where a plant grows: its soil, water, light and temperature. Understanding these conditions helps explain its behavior. We can then use that knowledge to design an environment in which selected conditions are controlled. A greenhouse with managed irrigation and lighting illustrates the engineering step.</p><p>In this analogy, harvesting is collecting an outcome, while harnessing is arranging a system so that its behavior serves a purpose. Both traditional and modern agriculture involve knowledge and engineering; the illustration isolates a distinction between understanding a phenomenon and deliberately controlling it. Quantum computing likewise requires more than observing a quantum effect: it requires a reproducible preparation, a controlled transformation and a useful readout.</p><figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/agriculture-analogy.png\" alt=\"An observed outdoor plant is compared with plants under controlled light, water and temperature in a greenhouse.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.3.</strong> Understanding conditions can guide the design of a controlled system. The agriculture comparison is an analogy, not a history of farming.<br><span class=\"image-source\">Source: Original illustration generated with AI assistance.</span></figcaption></figure><h3>The flight analogy</h3><p>Consider flight as an engineering analogy. Studying how a bird flies can reveal physical principles. Building an aircraft requires turning an understanding of those principles into a controlled system. Likewise, a quantum phenomenon becomes useful for computation only when we can prepare, manipulate and measure a system in a sufficiently controlled way. The analogy motivates the engineering question; it does not explain the mathematics of a qubit.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/flight-analogy.png\" alt=\"A bird, an early biplane and a passenger aircraft show observation, working demonstrations and reliable engineering.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.4.</strong> Technologies use physical laws without copying nature exactly. The stages illustrate an engineering analogy, not a schedule for quantum computing.<br><span class=\"image-source\">Source: Original illustration generated with AI assistance.</span></figcaption></figure><h3>The computational abstraction</h3><figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/computing-abstraction.svg\" alt=\"The learning task connects to states, gates and measurement, and ultimately to hardware through a software and control stack.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.5.</strong> Abstraction gives us a usable computational model. Device constraints still affect what we can implement and how well it works.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Our computational language has three recurring objects: <strong>states</strong> describe a preparation, <strong>operations</strong> change it, and <strong>measurements</strong> specify the possible recorded outcomes and their probabilities. We will study simple classical versions first, then make the quantum rules explicit. This lets us see both the similarities and the important changes.</p>\n<p>You do not need to know how to fabricate a quantum processor to follow this language. At the same time, implementation constraints matter when we evaluate a real experiment. Noise, connectivity and the amount of repeated measurement can affect the cost and reliability of the computation.</p></section>\n<section id=\"qubits\"><h2>5. Superposition: a first look at a qubit</h2>\n<p>A classical bit has labels 0 and 1. A pure qubit is described by a normalized complex state vector in a two-dimensional space. Using basis labels that we will explain in detail later, write</p>\n\\[|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\\]\n<p>The coefficients are <strong>amplitudes</strong>. For a measurement in the computational, or Z, basis, the probabilities of the recorded labels are \\(P(0)=|\\alpha|^2\\) and \\(P(1)=|\\beta|^2\\). One execution of this measurement records one outcome, 0 or 1. We estimate its probabilities by repeating a known preparation and measurement, not by reading all coefficients from one qubit.</p>\n<p>A state with both coefficients nonzero is a superposition relative to this basis. Merely having a 50/50 outcome distribution does not distinguish coherent superposition from a classical random preparation. Relative phase and its effect on later operations will give us more information.</p>\n<h3>The Bloch sphere</h3>\n<p>After omitting a physically irrelevant overall phase for the isolated state, we can represent a pure qubit as</p>\n\\[|\\psi\\rangle=\\cos(\\theta/2)|0\\rangle+e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)|1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi.\\]\n<p>The angle \\(\\theta\\) is polar and \\(\\phi\\) is azimuthal. The corresponding Bloch coordinates are</p>\n\\[(x,y,z)=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\ \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\ \\cos\\theta).\\]\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/qubit-bloch-sphere.svg\" alt=\"A projected Bloch sphere with x, y and z axes, a state vector, polar angle theta and azimuthal angle phi. Formulae alongside give its computational-basis measurement probabilities.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.6.</strong> The surface represents pure single-qubit states after omitting global phase. The Bloch coordinates are not the two probability amplitudes.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Changing \\(\\theta\\) changes the Z measurement probabilities. Changing \\(\\phi\\) at fixed \\(\\theta\\) changes relative phase while leaving those probabilities fixed. At either pole the azimuthal angle does not identify a different physical state. This picture represents a pure single qubit; a general multi-qubit state needs a larger description.</p>\n<p>Be careful about the geometry. The Bloch coordinate z is not an amplitude. The probabilities obey \\(P(0)=(1+z)/2\\) and \\(P(1)=(1-z)/2\\). The vector and equations should be read together.</p>\n<h3>What does Schrödinger’s cat ask us to think about?</h3>\n<p>The cat is a thought experiment about linking a quantum process to a macroscopic record. In an idealized apparatus, a decay event is connected to a detector and a trigger whose state affects the cat. A joint quantum description can associate different event components with different apparatus and cat descriptions.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/schrodinger-cat.svg\" alt=\"An idealized radioactive source, detector and trigger are linked to a cat in a sealed apparatus. No-decay and decay branches have different macroscopic records.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.7.</strong> Schrödinger’s thought experiment asks how a quantum description connects to a definite observed record. Merely not knowing what is inside a box does not establish coherent superposition.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The question is how this description relates to the definite record we observe. The schematic does not establish that an ordinary unopened box contains a coherent superposition. An unknown classical result in an envelope is a different situation. Nor should the cat be treated as an elementary model of how a processor stores a qubit.</p>\n<p>For our computations, we will state the preparation and measurement rules precisely and calculate their predictions. Questions about measurement and macroscopic records can then be explored with a clear mathematical model, rather than asking an analogy to carry the whole explanation.</p></section>\n<section id=\"interference\"><h2>6. Interference: how a gate makes phase observable</h2>\n<p>Closed-system quantum gates act linearly on state vectors and preserve their norm. A gate's matrix U is unitary: \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). Measurement has its own rules and should not be confused with applying such a gate.</p>\n<p>To see why phase matters, compare the two pure states</p>\n\\[|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad |-\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\\]\n<p>Both give 0 and 1 with equal probabilities in a Z measurement. Their relative signs differ. Apply the Hadamard gate H, whose action on the two basis states is \\(H|0\\rangle=|+\\rangle\\) and \\(H|1\\rangle=|-\\rangle\\). Expanding the sums gives</p>\n\\[H|+\\rangle=\\frac{(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)+(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)}{2}=|0\\rangle,\\]\n\\[H|-\\rangle=\\frac{(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)-(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)}{2}=|1\\rangle.\\]\n<p>We have combined amplitudes before squaring their magnitudes. One output amplitude cancels and the other survives. This is <strong>interference</strong>. H has not performed a measurement: it has changed the state so that the subsequent measurement distinguishes these inputs.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/phase-interference.svg\" alt=\"An equal superposition with relative phase zero or pi is acted on by a final Hadamard. Amplitude cancellation yields outcome zero or one respectively.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.8.</strong> Relative phase is invisible to the intermediate Z measurement in these examples, but a final gate changes what is observable.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In Figure 1.8, the phase gate is \\(P(\\phi)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\phi})\\): it leaves the 0 component unchanged and multiplies the 1 component by \\(e^{i\\phi}\\). Applying it after the first H prepares the displayed intermediate state.</p>\n<p>For an adjustable phase, start with \\((|0\\rangle+e^{i\\phi}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). After H, the amplitudes are \\((1+e^{i\\phi})/2\\) and \\((1-e^{i\\phi})/2\\). Thus the final probability of 1 is \\((1-\\cos\\phi)/2\\), although the earlier Z probabilities were both 1/2. This gives us a continuous version of the two examples.</p>\n<div data-opening-lab=\"phase\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"intro-phase\"></div>\n<p>This is a small ingredient of quantum computation, not a general shortcut for optimization. A useful algorithm must arrange its transformations so that accessible measurement statistics answer the specified question, with the required accuracy and resource cost.</p></section>\n<section id=\"entanglement\"><h2>7. Entanglement: why describe the whole system?</h2>\n<p>For two independent pure-state preparations, a product description combines their individual state vectors. But not every joint pure state can be written that way. A standard example is the Bell state</p>\n\\[|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\\]\n<p>Suppose it were a product \\((a|0\\rangle+b|1\\rangle)(c|0\\rangle+d|1\\rangle)\\), with the product understood as a tensor product. Matching its four coefficients would require \\(ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(ad=bc=0\\). The first two conditions make all four factors nonzero; the latter conditions cannot then hold. This contradiction shows that the pure state does not factor. Such a state is <strong>entangled</strong>.</p>\n<p>Measuring both qubits in Z gives matching outcomes, 00 or 11, each with probability 1/2. But an equal classical mixture of 00 and 11 gives those same Z statistics. Matching results in one basis are therefore not enough to establish entanglement.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/entanglement-correlations.svg\" alt=\"A coherent Bell state and an equal classical mixture of 00 and 11 have the same matching Z outcomes, but different matching probabilities in X measurements.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.9.</strong> One-basis correlation is insufficient to identify an entangled state. Here X measurement can be implemented by applying H before the usual Z readout.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>If both qubits of this Bell state are measured in X instead, their outcomes still match with probability one. For the classical mixture, those X outcomes match with probability 1/2. Here an X measurement means applying H before the usual Z readout on each qubit. We will derive the full basis-change and joint-measurement rules in the later foundations.</p>\n<p>These correlations do not provide a controllable faster-than-light message. A party cannot choose its local measurement outcome to encode the desired bit. Comparing separated records requires ordinary communication. We will distinguish local statistics, joint correlations and conditional descriptions carefully.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"intro-correlation\"></div><h3>What can entanglement be used for?</h3><p><strong>Superdense coding:</strong> Alice and Bob initially share a Bell pair. Alice encodes one of four messages by applying an appropriate local gate, then physically sends her qubit to Bob. A joint measurement can distinguish the four messages, conveying two classical bits using one transmitted qubit and a previously shared entangled pair. The shared pair and the transmitted qubit are both resources.</p><p><strong>Teleportation:</strong> an unknown qubit state can be transferred using a shared Bell pair, a joint measurement by Alice, two classical message bits and a conditional correction by Bob. The original state is not retained as an extra copy. Bob needs the classical message to reconstruct the state; the protocol cannot deliver a usable message faster than light.</p><p><strong>Key distribution:</strong> some protocols use entangled pairs to establish and test correlations. Matching outcomes alone do not certify a secret key: authentication, statistical tests, error correction and privacy amplification are essential parts of a security argument.</p><p>These examples show why we call entanglement a resource. Let us now use it in a completely specified task whose classical and quantum performances we can calculate.</p></section>\n<section id=\"chsh\"><h2>8. The CHSH game: a measurable difference</h2>\n<p>Imagine two players, Alice and Bob, in separate rooms. Before a round they may agree on a strategy and share resources. Once the round starts, they cannot communicate. A referee independently chooses uniformly random bits x and y, sends x only to Alice and y only to Bob, and receives one output bit a from Alice and b from Bob.</p>\n\\[a\\oplus b=x\\land y.\\]\n<p>They win when their answers have the same parity as the AND of their questions. In ordinary language: answer the same unless both inputs are 1; in that case answer differently. Neither player knows the other’s input.</p>\n<table><caption>The four equally likely input pairs</caption><thead><tr><th>Alice x</th><th>Bob y</th><th>Required outputs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Different</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/chsh-information.svg\" alt=\"Referee sends private bits to Alice and Bob, who return local outputs without exchanging messages.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.10.</strong> The referee compares the records; the shared pair does not carry a chosen message between players.</figcaption></figure><h3>Why the best classical success probability is 75%</h3><p>A deterministic local strategy specifies four answers: a₀, a₁, b₀ and b₁. Winning all four cases would require</p>\n\\[a_0\\oplus b_0=0,\\quad a_0\\oplus b_1=0,\\quad a_1\\oplus b_0=0,\\quad a_1\\oplus b_1=1.\\]\n<p>XOR all four equations. Every answer occurs twice on the left, so the left side is 0, but the right side is 1. This is impossible. At least one input pair must lose. Always answering 0 wins three of the four, so the maximum is exactly 3/4. Shared classical randomness only mixes deterministic strategies; averaging strategies whose scores are at most 3/4 cannot exceed that bound.</p>\n<h3>The quantum strategy</h3><p>Here Z distinguishes the computational basis and X distinguishes the plus/minus basis. Their matrices are \\(Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\). A measurement of either has outcomes +1 and −1; the chosen direction determines which state components it distinguishes.</p><p>Before separating, the players share the Bell state \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\). Alice measures Z when x=0 and X when x=1. Bob measures \\((Z+X)/\\sqrt2\\) when y=0 and \\((Z-X)/\\sqrt2\\) when y=1. These are different measurement directions, not instructions to measure both X and Z in the same round. Outcome +1 becomes bit 0; outcome −1 becomes bit 1.</p>\n<p>Let \\(E_{xy}=\\mathbb{E}[A_xB_y]\\), with \\(A_x,B_y\\in\\{-1,+1\\}\\). For this Bell state, the expectations of ZZ and XX are 1, while those of ZX and XZ are 0. Linearity therefore gives</p>\n\\[E_{00}=E_{01}=E_{10}=1/\\sqrt2,\\qquad E_{11}=-1/\\sqrt2.\\]\n<p>Define \\(S=E_{00}+E_{01}+E_{10}-E_{11}\\). Give a round score +1 for a win and −1 for a loss. Uniform inputs make the average score S/4, and also make it \\(2P(\\mathrm{win})-1\\). Hence</p>\n\\[P(\\mathrm{win})=\\frac12+\\frac S8=\\frac{2+\\sqrt2}{4}=\\cos^2(\\pi/8)\\approx0.8536.\\]\n<p>For these inputs and rules this quantum strategy attains the optimal quantum value. It is better than 75%, but does not guarantee a win. Each player’s local outcomes remain uniformly random; useful correlations appear when the referee compares the records. No message passes between the players during a round.</p>\n<div data-opening-lab=\"chsh\"></div><div data-quiz=\"intro-chsh\"></div>\n<p><strong>What did we establish?</strong> A precise advantage for an entanglement-assisted correlation task under specified rules. This does not prove that all quantum algorithms are faster, or that any particular QML model learns better. For the theoretical background, see <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.23.880\">Clauser, Horne, Shimony and Holt (1969)</a> and <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00417500\">Cirel’son’s quantum bound (1980)</a>.</p></section>\n<section id=\"evolution\"><h2>9. From ideal algorithms to useful machines</h2>\n<p>Once quantum theory gives us a computational model, a sequence of engineering questions follows. It is useful to view these as overlapping strands of development rather than rigid eras that ended on particular dates.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/computing-evolution.svg\" alt=\"Ideal algorithms, reliable computation, physical experiments and useful systems pose overlapping research questions.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.11.</strong> Each question adds requirements to the claim of a useful computation.</figcaption></figure><h3>Can the ideal model do something valuable?</h3><p>Feynman’s <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02650179\">1982 paper on simulating physics</a> helped articulate the motivation for quantum simulation. Shor’s 1994 factoring and discrete-logarithm algorithms and Grover’s 1996 search algorithm provided striking algorithmic examples. The type of improvement differs by task, and depends on the comparison model. An ideal algorithm is a mathematical result; implementing it at useful scale is another question.</p>\n<h3>Can the computation survive errors?</h3><p>A physical qubit is not an ideal vector isolated from the world. Imperfect gates, interactions with the environment and readout errors change the observed statistics. Quantum error correction distributes logical information over physical systems and diagnoses errors without simply reading out the unknown logical state. It cannot be understood as making ordinary independent copies of an arbitrary qubit.</p>\n<p>A <strong>logical qubit</strong> is encoded information protected by a code; a <strong>physical qubit</strong> is a hardware resource used to implement that encoding and its checks. There is no universal fixed conversion ratio. Code choice, error rates, connectivity and the desired computation determine the overhead. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9702058\">Fault-tolerance threshold results</a> show how reliable computation can be possible under appropriate error assumptions and below a threshold; they do not remove the engineering cost.</p>\n<h3>What can limited noisy devices demonstrate?</h3><p>The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.00862\">noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) perspective</a> emphasizes machines with limited qubit counts and circuit depth. A sampling experiment can demonstrate a difficult computational task without solving a commercially useful problem. Conversely, a promising application needs evidence that its accuracy, reliability and full costs meet a practical requirement. These claims must be distinguished.</p>\n<h3>How do we make the entire system useful?</h3><p>Algorithms, compilers, controls, error correction and hardware must work together. A compiler can change the gate count; connectivity can introduce routing operations; better controls can change error rates; a different encoding can change readout costs. Progress is therefore a systems problem. Physical qubit count alone does not summarize a machine’s usable computing power.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway.</strong> The progression is from a mathematical possibility, through reliable implementation, towards useful performance at scale. Work on these strands proceeds together.</p></section>\n<section id=\"evaluation\"><h2>10. How should we evaluate a quantum computation?</h2>\n<p>A performance claim becomes meaningful only after specifying the task and the success criterion. “Faster” might mean fewer oracle calls, shorter circuit execution, lower training time or a shorter complete workflow. These measurements can lead to different conclusions.</p>\n<table><caption>Questions behind a useful comparison</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What to specify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What is the task?</td><td>Input distribution, output, problem size and required accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>What is counted?</td><td>Preparation, preprocessing, circuit executions, classical processing, readout and retries.</td></tr><tr><td>How reliable is the answer?</td><td>Success probability, uncertainty, noise and the number of repetitions.</td></tr><tr><td>What is the baseline?</td><td>A suitable classical method with comparable accuracy and a fair resource budget.</td></tr><tr><td>How does it scale?</td><td>The dependence on problem size, precision and error-correction overhead.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For example, suppose one measurement produces a 0/1 result with probability p of 1. Averaging M independent repetitions estimates p with standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/M}\\). In this elementary sampling scheme, reducing that uncertainty by a factor of two requires four times as many repetitions. A circuit that is short per execution can still be expensive when training requires many such estimates.</p>\n<p>For QML, add the learning questions: were test examples kept separate, were preprocessing and parameter choices fair, and does a reported improvement persist across meaningful repetitions? More expressive state descriptions do not by themselves establish better generalization.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"intro-evaluation\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"qml-connection\"><h2>11. Return to the quantum learning loop</h2>\n<p>We can now name the pieces of a possible quantum learning experiment. An encoding prepares a state from data. Gates, some with adjustable parameters, transform it. Repeated preparations and measurements estimate an output. Classical processing turns that estimate into a prediction and a loss. An optimizer proposes new parameters and the process repeats.</p>\n<p>Both sides of this connection matter. How expensive is preparing the data? How many circuit evaluations does training require? How much measurement is needed to estimate the output accurately? Does the model generalize beyond its training examples? What classical model and computational budget provide a fair comparison?</p>\n<p>Quantum computation can offer different resources for particular tasks, but a superposition alone does not guarantee a speedup. An algorithmic query comparison, the runtime of an implementation and the usefulness of a complete learning workflow are different claims. We will state the assumptions behind each claim we study.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"intro-purpose\"></div>\n</section>\n<section id=\"next\"><h2>12. From this first picture to a working foundation</h2>\n<p>This chapter has introduced several ideas early so you can see where we are going. You are not expected to have mastered their full notation yet. The next chapter starts with switches, dice and envelopes, where we can develop state descriptions and operation matrices with familiar examples. We then introduce complex numbers, amplitudes, measurement and multi-qubit calculations in a systematic way.</p>\n<p>Use these questions to check the conceptual picture before continuing:</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Change the game.</strong> Verify that the classical answers a₀=a₁=b₀=b₁=0 win three CHSH cases. What happens if the referee always sends 00 instead of choosing uniformly?<details><summary>Worked reasoning</summary><p>The fixed strategy loses only on 11. If 00 is always sent, the same strategy wins every round. The 75% bound depends on the stated uniform independent inputs, as well as locality.</p></details></li><li><strong>Map a learning task.</strong> Choose a classification problem. Identify the data, target, prediction and loss, then describe where a quantum circuit could be proposed. What comparison would you need?\n<details><summary>Worked reasoning</summary><p>For a binary task, classical features x and labels y might feed an encoding and parameterized circuit; measurement estimates contribute to a prediction and a classical loss. This specifies a candidate model, not its value. We still need a defined data split, an appropriate classical baseline and resource accounting for preparation, training and readout.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Separate the observations.</strong> Why can the states plus and minus agree in Z probabilities but differ after H?\n<details><summary>Worked reasoning</summary><p>Z probabilities square the magnitudes of the separate coefficients and omit their relative sign. H adds and subtracts the amplitudes before the final squaring. That sign then changes which amplitude cancels, as the worked calculation shows.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Locate the boundary of an analogy.</strong> What does a sealed envelope explain, and what does it not establish?\n<details><summary>Worked reasoning</summary><p>It explains uncertainty about a classical record and an information update after reading it. It does not establish coherent superposition or reproduce all the state-transformation and measurement statistics of a quantum system.</p></details></li></ol>\n<p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#classical-information\">Continue to Chapter 2 · Classical Systems and Information</a></p></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-information", "title": "Classical Systems and Information", "number": 2, "part": "Part A · Quantum Computing Foundations", "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/overview.html", "lessons": ["classical-states", "classical-probability", "classical-operations", "classical-composition"], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Before asking what makes a quantum computer different, let us build a language for systems we already understand. A switch, a die and a sealed result envelope will take us from state labels to probability columns, operation matrices and complete calculations.</p>\n<p>The chapter follows the lecture's systems-first approach: ask what information we need, choose a representation, work through a small example, and explain what the answer means. The theory, derivations and worked reasoning form the main reading path. Experiments and programming let us check our predictions.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Before we begin</strong><p>Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra are enough to start. We explain coordinate conventions as we use them and include a matrix-multiplication refresher before composition. No prior quantum physics or Qiskit experience is required.</p></aside>\n<section id=\"goals\"><h2>What questions will we be able to answer?</h2><ul><li>How can a four-setting switch use a four-component basis column and a two-bit code?</li><li>What does a probability vector say about a sealed result, and what changes when information arrives?</li><li>How can we build an operation matrix from its response to each definite input?</li><li>Why does applying A and then B give BA, and why can reversing the devices change the answer?</li></ul></section>\n<section id=\"sequence\"><h2>Follow the reasoning through four lessons</h2><ol class=\"chapter-lessons\"><li><a href=\"#classical-states\">Finite systems, states and basis labels</a><p>Start with switches and a die. Choose labels, declare their order and distinguish coordinates from binary encoding.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-probability\">Probability distributions and repeated observations</a><p>Open the sealed-envelope example, question a probability model, then work events, samples and conditional probabilities.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-operations\">Deterministic and stochastic operations</a><p>Build the four bit functions and the lecture's asymmetric random rule. Derive their action on an uncertain input.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-composition\">Composing operations</a><p>Follow intermediate states, combine devices and work out why their order matters.</p></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"study\"><h2>Predict first, then calculate and compare</h2><p>When a question appears in the text, try the next step before revealing the working. In each experiment you can choose the inputs, predict the result and open the calculation. The probability sampler also lets you compare a fixed model with fresh batches. The joint-table experiment shows what changes after learning one variable.</p><p>The eight concept checks give feedback for each option. End-of-lesson exercises include worked reasoning and hints where useful. Check counts are saved in this browser as a study aid; they do not certify mastery or restrict navigation. The revised envelope and asymmetric-operation questions have their own check records.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/Ashutosh-Bhatia/quantum-machine-learning/blob/main/notebooks/00-classical-information.ipynb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python and NumPy companion ↗</a> follows the same examples: predict, calculate by hand, run and explain. Qiskit enters with quantum circuits. You can study the written chapter fully without running the notebook.</p><p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#classical-states\">Begin Lesson 2.1</a></p></section>\n<section id=\"review\"><h2>What comes next</h2><p>We will revisit these classical descriptions when the rules change to amplitudes and quantum measurement, and return to the four bit functions in Deutsch’s problem. Part A develops quantum computing, Part B builds the classical ML bridge, and Part C brings them together in QML.</p></section>\n"}, {"id": "mathematical-language", "title": "Mathematical Language for Quantum Computing", "number": 3, "part": "Part A · Quantum Computing Foundations", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/overview.html", "lessons": ["math-complex", "math-dot-norm", "math-inner", "math-outer", "math-matrices", "math-unitary", "math-tensor"], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">We already know how to label a classical state and apply a matrix to a column. This chapter supplies the mathematical language needed when the entries become complex amplitudes: magnitude and phase, inner products, projectors, unitary maps and tensor products.</p><section id=\"route\"><h2>Learn the calculation, then use it</h2><p>We will not ask unfamiliar notation to explain itself. Each lesson starts with a concrete calculation, develops its general rule, and gives you a way to predict and inspect the result. Figures support the written reasoning; the activities do not replace it.</p><p>Begin with complex numbers, then build the real dot product and norm. Conjugation leads to complex inner products and bras. Outer products give projectors; matrices give transformations; unitaries identify norm-preserving gates; tensor products combine spaces. The same ideas return in Chapter 4’s state and measurement rules.</p><p>Basic algebra and Chapter 2’s columns are sufficient to begin. Trigonometric functions are introduced through angles; you can study the Cartesian calculations before exploring phase.</p></section><section id=\"lessons\"><h2>The seven lessons</h2><ol class=\"chapter-lessons\"><li><a href=\"#math-complex\">Lesson 3.1 · Complex numbers, magnitude and phase</a><p>Compute conjugates, products and squared magnitudes.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-dot-norm\">Lesson 3.2 · Dot products, norms and projections</a><p>Calculate real dot products and connect their signs to angles.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-inner\">Lesson 3.3 · Bras, kets and complex inner products</a><p>Construct conjugate transposes and calculate inner products.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-outer\">Lesson 3.4 · Outer products and projectors</a><p>Distinguish scalar inner products from matrix outer products.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-matrices\">Lesson 3.5 · Matrices, linear maps and adjoints</a><p>Interpret matrix columns as transformed basis vectors.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-unitary\">Lesson 3.6 · Unitary matrices and preserved geometry</a><p>Verify unitarity by adjoint multiplication and column inner products.</p></li><li><a href=\"#math-tensor\">Lesson 3.7 · Tensor products and combined systems</a><p>Expand vector and operator tensor products with explicit ordering.</p></li></ol></section><section id=\"method\"><h2>Predict, calculate, explore and explain</h2><p>Before moving a slider or pressing a step button, predict a sign, a dimension or a likely result. Calculate a small case by hand, compare with the activity, and explain any disagreement. The animations are controlled by you; no activity starts playing automatically. Each concept check gives reasoning for every choice.</p><p>The companion notebook uses Python and NumPy to reproduce the calculations. It requires no quantum hardware account. Qiskit is unnecessary for these linear-algebra foundations; it enters when we build circuits.</p><p><a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/Ashutosh-Bhatia/quantum-machine-learning/blob/main/notebooks/04-mathematical-language.ipynb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the chapter notebook in Colab ↗</a></p></section><section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>A normalized complex state.</strong> For \\(\\psi=((1+2i)/3,-2/3)^{\\mathsf T}\\), calculate its norm, bra and computational-basis squared magnitudes.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Add the two squared magnitudes; conjugate each entry for the bra.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The squared norm is 5/9+4/9=1. Its bra is \\(((1-2i)/3,-2/3)\\); the squared magnitudes are 5/9 and 4/9.</p></details></li><li><strong>Two kinds of products.</strong> For \\(u=(1,i)^{\\mathsf T}/\\sqrt2\\), compute \\(u^\\dagger u\\) and \\(uu^\\dagger\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Keep track of the output dimensions before multiplying.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The first is the scalar 1. The second is \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-i\\\\i&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\), a projector. Check its square.</p></details></li><li><strong>A complex projector.</strong> For that u and v=(1,0), find Pv and its squared norm, where \\(P=uu^\\dagger\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Apply the projector matrix to the basis column.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The output is (1,i)/2 with squared norm 1/2. Its normalized branch is u.</p></details></li><li><strong>An error to diagnose.</strong> A learner writes \\(\\|(1,i)\\|^2=1+i^2=0\\). Identify and repair the error.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Use conjugation in the self-product.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>They used squares instead of squared magnitudes. The result must be \\(1+|i|^2=2\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Combine and transform.</strong> Find \\((H\\otimes I)(|0\\rangle\\otimes|1\\rangle)\\) and check its norm.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Use (A⊗B)(u⊗v)=Au⊗Bv.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The output is \\((|01\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\), whose squared norm is 1/2+1/2=1.</p></details></li><li><strong>Explain the scope.</strong> Why is a projector useful in quantum mathematics even though it is not usually unitary?<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Distinguish component extraction from reversible evolution.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It extracts a component used in measurement calculations. A conditional branch and its probability require additional rules; it is not a reversible closed-system gate.</p></details></li></ol></section><section id=\"next\"><h2>What comes next</h2><p>Chapter 4 develops qubits and measurement using this language. You will already know how to normalize amplitudes, calculate overlaps and separate a projected branch from its probability. Return to the relevant worked calculation whenever notation feels unfamiliar.</p><p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#math-complex\">Begin Lesson 3.1</a></p></section>"}], "readingOrder": ["preface", "quantum-introduction", "classical-information", "classical-states", "classical-probability", "classical-operations", "classical-composition", "mathematical-language", "math-complex", "math-dot-norm", "math-inner", "math-outer", "math-matrices", "math-unitary", "math-tensor"], "lessons": [{"id": "classical-states", "number": "2.1", "title": "Finite systems, states and basis labels", "part": "Part A · Chapter 2", "objectives": ["Choose a finite state set appropriate to a modelling question.", "Translate a definite state into a labelled standard basis vector.", "Distinguish coordinate dimension from binary storage size."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/1-states.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "states-order", "question": "If we reorder the coordinates, does the same switch setting necessarily keep the same column vector?", "options": ["Yes: coordinate order has no effect.", "No: the coordinates can change while the labelled setting stays the same.", "No: reordering physically changes the setting."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["The position of the 1 depends on which coordinate corresponds to the setting.", "Correct. A vector must be interpreted with its declared basis order.", "Changing our coordinate convention does not operate on the physical device."]}, {"id": "states-capacity", "question": "What is the minimum fixed-length binary storage for five distinct labels?", "options": ["Two bits", "Three bits", "Five bits"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Two bits provide only four distinct strings.", "Correct. Three bits provide eight strings; three are unused in a five-label code.", "Five one-hot coordinates are one representation, but they are not the minimum number of storage bits."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. State labels, coordinate order and ket notation are explained here.", "chapter_id": "classical-information", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Before we discuss a quantum computer, let us ask a simpler question: what do we need to know about a system to describe its state? We can build the language for this question with a switch and a die, and then use it to understand computation.</p>\n<section id=\"systems\"><h2>1. Start with a system we already understand</h2>\n<p>Consider an on/off switch. If our question is simply which position has been selected, there are two possible answers: off and on. For a die resting on a table, we might ask which face is uppermost. That gives six possible answers. A switch with off, low, medium and high settings gives four.</p>\n<p>We call the collection of possible classical state labels the <strong>state set</strong>, denoted by \\(\\Sigma\\). For these examples,</p>\n\\[\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{bit}}=\\{0,1\\},\\quad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{die}}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\quad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{switch}}=\\{\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{medium},\\mathrm{high}\\}.\\]\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/state-sets.svg\" alt=\"An on/off switch has two labels, a resting die six face labels, and a four-setting control four labels.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.1a.</strong> Choose the question before choosing the state labels. The object can have many other properties that this model does not record.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Notice the modelling decision we made. A real switch also has a temperature and a physical position; a die has an orientation in space. We have selected the distinctions needed for our question. If the question changes, the state description may need to change too. Describing a switch by its selected setting is enough to record that setting, but it would not be enough to predict the dynamics of a motor connected to it.</p>\n<p>We can now see a simple model of computation. A system is in some state; an operation acts on it; the system has an output state. An on/off switch is a small example. A computer memory has many more possible states, but the question is still familiar: what is the state, and how does an operation change it?</p>\n<p>Try giving a state set for another familiar device before continuing. State what your labels describe. The important first step is choosing the question, not choosing complicated mathematics.</p></section>\n<section id=\"labels\"><h2>2. Give each label a coordinate</h2>\n<p>Suppose our four-setting switch is set to medium. We could write the word “medium” and stop there. Why introduce a vector? Because we want a common mathematical language for definite states, uncertainty about states, and operations on states. A column representation will let us build that language piece by piece.</p>\n<p>Choose the coordinate order \\((\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{medium},\\mathrm{high})\\). Put a 1 in the coordinate corresponding to the selected label and zeros in the other coordinates:</p>\n\\[e_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{low}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\\\0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{medium}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{high}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is a <strong>one-hot representation</strong>. The column for medium selects the third position in our declared list. We have not measured a new physical quantity by writing four numbers; we have represented the label in coordinates.</p>\n<h3>Pause and predict: what if we reverse the list?</h3>\n<p>Keep the switch at medium, but use the order (high, medium, low, off). Where should the 1 go now?</p>\n<details><summary>Work through the change</summary><p>Medium is the second label in the new list, so its column is \\((0,1,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). The old column was \\((0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Both describe medium, with different coordinate conventions. Reordering the list does not operate on the switch.</p></details>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/coordinate-order.svg\" alt=\"The same medium setting has column (0,0,1,0) in off-low-medium-high order and (0,1,0,0) in reversed order.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.1b.</strong> Reordering coordinates changes the column, not the selected setting. The separately assigned binary code stays 10.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Keep this habit throughout the course: write the order before interpreting a column. Later, the order of qubits in a circuit diagram and the order of symbols in a ket will need the same care.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-order\"></div>\n<h3>Does a die have to start at label zero?</h3>\n<p>No. The physical face label 1 and a programming array position 0 are different things. We can choose the ordered face labels (1,2,3,4,5,6); the column for face 1 is then \\((1,0,0,0,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). A program may store its first entry at array index 0. The mapping between label and position must be stated; it should never be guessed from the number printed on the die.</p></section>\n<section id=\"basis\"><h2>3. Basis vectors and the ket language</h2>\n<p>For a finite state set with \\(d\\) labels, we construct \\(d\\) standard coordinate vectors. Each has a single 1 in its corresponding coordinate. These form the <strong>standard basis</strong>: every \\(d\\)-component vector can be written uniquely as a weighted sum of them.</p>\n<p>Let us see what that statement means for the switch. If</p>\n\\[v=\\begin{pmatrix}v_{\\mathrm{off}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{low}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{medium}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{high}}\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>then</p>\n\\[v=v_{\\mathrm{off}}e_{\\mathrm{off}}+v_{\\mathrm{low}}e_{\\mathrm{low}}+v_{\\mathrm{medium}}e_{\\mathrm{medium}}+v_{\\mathrm{high}}e_{\\mathrm{high}}.\\]\n<p>Each term contributes to only one coordinate. Adding the terms reconstructs the original column. The coefficients are unique because no other basis vector contributes to that coordinate. This simple observation is why the representation is useful: we can understand a linear operation by understanding its action on the basis columns.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/basis-reconstruction.svg\" alt=\"Weighted basis columns 2 e_off, 1 e_low, 3 e_medium and 0 e_high add to the vector (2,1,3,0).\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.1c.</strong> A weighted sum reconstructs a vector one coordinate at a time. These illustrative weights demonstrate algebra; they are not probabilities.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>We also write \\(|a\\rangle\\) for the basis column associated with label \\(a\\), and read it as “ket a.” For a bit, in order (0,1),</p>\n\\[|0\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad |1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The symbols may look unfamiliar, but here they are names for columns we already understand. Using a ket does not by itself make a description quantum. In the next lesson, a weighted sum will describe classical uncertainty, and the coefficients will be probabilities. When we reach quantum states, the coefficients and the measurement rule will change. We will explain that change explicitly.</p></section>\n<section id=\"encoding\"><h2>4. Four coordinates do not mean four storage bits</h2>\n<p>How many bits do we need to record the setting of our four-setting switch? Two bits give the strings 00, 01, 10 and 11. We can assign one string to each setting: off ↦ 00, low ↦ 01, medium ↦ 10, high ↦ 11. Every setting has a different code.</p>\n<p>Compare this with the one-hot column for medium: it has four entries, but its binary label code has two digits. The coordinate representation and the storage encoding answer different questions. The column provides a basis for calculations; the binary string stores which label was selected.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/state-representations.svg\" alt=\"The four switch settings off, low, medium and high have four-component one-hot columns and distinct two-bit codes 00, 01, 10 and 11.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.1d.</strong> One selected setting, two representations. The basis order is (off, low, medium, high); the four-component column and the two-bit code have different roles.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In general, \\(n\\) bits provide \\(2^n\\) different strings: each position has two choices. To encode \\(d\\) distinct labels with fixed-length strings, we need</p>\n\\[2^n\\ge d,\\qquad n_{\\min}=\\lceil\\log_2d\\rceil.\\]\n<p>The ceiling means round up. If logarithms are unfamiliar, ask how many times we must double 1 before reaching at least \\(d\\). A one-label system needs no information-bearing bit to distinguish alternatives, since there are none.</p>\n<h3>An extension: when a code has unused strings</h3>\n<p>Now suppose a different switch has only three settings: off, low and high. One bit is insufficient because it gives only two strings. Two bits still suffice, but now we can use 00, 01 and 10 and leave 11 unused. That unused code does not automatically create another physical setting; an application must decide how to handle it.</p>\n<p>A six-state die similarly needs three fixed-length binary digits. Two digits give only four strings; three give eight, leaving two unused. None of this says that the die occupies all its possible faces at once. We have counted labels and assigned codes.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"states\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-capacity\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Work it through yourself</h2>\n<p>Reading a column and constructing one yourself are different tasks. Attempt these before opening the hints or worked reasoning.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>The four-setting switch.</strong> In order (off, low, medium, high), write the column and fixed binary code for medium using the code defined above. Explain why the column length differs from the code length.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Find medium's position in the coordinate list, then use its assigned binary string. These are separate steps.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column is \\((0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\); the code is 10. Four basis coordinates provide a vector representation of the four labels, while two binary digits provide four different strings for storing a label.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Reorder without operating on the device.</strong> Rewrite the medium column in order (high, medium, low, off). Does the fixed code 10 change?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column becomes \\((0,1,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). The fixed code remains 10 because we changed the coordinate list, not the label-to-code assignment or the switch setting. A new code assignment would be a separate convention change.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Face label or array position?</strong> For face order (1,2,3,4,5,6), write the column for face 3. Which entry would a zero-indexed array select?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column is \\((0,0,1,0,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Face 3 occupies the third list position, which is array index 2. A numerical face label need not equal its storage index.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Five messages.</strong> Find the minimum fixed-length binary storage, give a possible code, and count unused strings.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Count the strings available with two digits and then with three.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Three bits suffice: two give only four strings, while three give eight. Use 000, 001, 010, 011 and 100, leaving three strings unused. A one-hot representation would instead have five coordinates.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we built?</strong><p>Choose the state labels, declare their order, and represent each definite label by a basis column. We now have a language for calculations. Next we ask how to use that same language when the system has a definite classical result but we do not know it.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-probability", "number": "2.2", "title": "Probability distributions and repeated observations", "part": "Part A · Chapter 2", "objectives": ["Validate a probability vector and calculate event probabilities.", "Separate a model probability from a finite empirical frequency.", "Calculate conditional probabilities from events and joint tables, including zero-probability boundaries."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/2-probability.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "prob-envelope", "question": "The envelope contains a recorded result, with pass labelled 0 and fail labelled 1. What does p=(3/4,1/4)ᵀ describe before opening it?", "options": ["Our probabilities for the two recorded labels.", "A coherent quantum superposition of pass and fail.", "A guarantee that opening four envelopes produces exactly one failure."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. It describes uncertainty about the classical label. Observing pass updates our information to (1,0)ᵀ without requiring the printed result to change.", "The ket/column notation alone does not make a classical letter a quantum system. The entries here are probabilities, not amplitudes.", "Expected proportions are not a guarantee about a finite batch. A batch also requires a specified repeated-preparation model."]}, {"id": "prob-frequency", "question": "A model has P(1)=0.25, but a 100-trial batch contains 28 ones. What follows?", "options": ["The exact probability must be 0.28.", "The observed frequency is 0.28; it can differ from the model probability.", "The model has been proved false."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["A finite frequency is an estimate, not a proof of the exact probability.", "Correct. Interpreting the size of the discrepancy also requires the sampling assumptions and an uncertainty analysis.", "This observation alone does not prove the model false. Finite samples fluctuate."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "prerequisites": "Lesson 2.1: ordered state labels, basis columns and their meaning.", "chapter_id": "classical-information", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Suppose a sealed envelope contains a result: pass or fail. The result has been recorded, but we have not opened the envelope. How can we describe what we know about it? This question takes us from a definite label to a probability vector.</p>\n<section id=\"distributions\"><h2>1. The envelope and our uncertainty</h2>\n<p>Let us label pass by 0 and fail by 1. Suppose our information is represented by probabilities 3/4 for pass and 1/4 for fail. In the coordinate order (0,1), we write</p>\n\\[p=\\frac34|0\\rangle+\\frac14|1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>What are these coefficients saying? They describe our uncertainty about the label inside the envelope. They do not say that the printed result is physically both pass and fail. If we open the envelope and find pass, our updated description is \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). We have learned the label. Opening the envelope need not have changed the result written on the letter.</p>\n<p>A probability vector can also describe a random preparation that produces different labels on different trials. In either case, we must state what the random variable and its probabilities refer to. An unopened fixed letter and a procedure for preparing many letters are different experiments, even if we use the same numerical column.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/classical-envelope.svg\" alt=\"A sealed result has prior probabilities 3/4 and 1/4. Observing fail updates the information to probabilities zero and one.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.2.</strong> An information update after reading a recorded result. The result on the letter need not change.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>The general definition</h3>\n<p>For ordered state labels \\((a_1,\\ldots,a_d)\\), let \\(p_j=\\Pr(X=a_j)\\). A complete distribution is a column satisfying</p>\n\\[p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_1\\\\\\vdots\\\\p_d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad p_j\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_j=1.\\]\n<p>The entries are nonnegative probabilities. Their sum is one because the labels describe mutually exclusive, exhaustive outcomes: precisely one of the labels applies on a trial. Thus \\((0.2,0.3)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is incomplete on a two-state set, while \\((1.2,-0.2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is invalid despite summing to one.</p>\n<p>A <strong>point mass</strong> records certainty about one label. Our basis columns \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) are the bit examples. Intermediate nonnegative weights describe uncertainty. In this chapter the coefficients themselves are probabilities; we do not square them. Quantum amplitudes and their measurement rule will be introduced later.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-envelope\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"events\"><h2>2. Ask a question about several outcomes</h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is a subset of the state set. Return to a die, now with distribution in face order 1 through 6:</p>\n\\[p=\\frac1{12}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\1\\\\3\\\\2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Before calculating, identify which labels answer the question “is the upper face even?” They are \\(E=\\{2,4,6\\}\\). Only one face is observed on a trial, so the routes are mutually exclusive and we add:</p>\n\\[\\Pr(E)=p_2+p_4+p_6=\\frac{2+3+3}{12}=\\frac23.\\]\n<p>We have not created a new physical face called “even.” We have asked a question that groups existing outcomes. The complement \\(E^c\\) contains the remaining labels, so \\(\\Pr(E^c)=1-\\Pr(E)\\).</p>\n<p>When two events overlap, simply adding their probabilities counts the intersection twice. The correct identity is</p>\n\\[\\Pr(A\\cup B)=\\Pr(A)+\\Pr(B)-\\Pr(A\\cap B).\\]\n<p>For disjoint events the intersection term vanishes. This is the condition that made the die-face calculation above valid.</p></section>\n<section id=\"frequency\"><h2>3. Where did the probabilities come from?</h2>\n<p>A model can specify probabilities, and observations can help us estimate them. But assigning a probability deserves care. Suppose a team won seven of its last ten matches. Does that make its probability of winning the next match exactly 0.7? What if strong players have retired, or the opposing team has changed?</p>\n<p>The historical frequency is a fact about those ten matches. Using it as the next-match probability introduces assumptions about how relevant those observations are. We should ask what conditions stayed the same and what changed. This is already a familiar concern in machine learning: a numerical estimate does not remove the need for a faithful model of the data.</p>\n<p>For a simpler calculation, assume a fixed bit-preparation model with \\(\\Pr(X=1)=1/4\\). Repeat that preparation independently \\(N\\) times under unchanged conditions. If \\(N_1\\) trials produce 1, the observed frequency is</p>\n\\[\\widehat p_1=\\frac{N_1}{N}.\\]\n<p>The hat distinguishes the finite-sample estimate from the model probability. With 100 trials the expected count is 25 ones. A batch might instead have 28, giving \\(\\widehat p_1=0.28\\). That observation does not prove that the exact probability is 0.28.</p>\n<h3>Work out the expected count</h3>\n<p>Let \\(I_t\\) be 1 when trial \\(t\\) produces a 1, and 0 otherwise. Then \\(\\mathbb E[I_t]=1\\cdot p_1+0\\cdot(1-p_1)=p_1\\). Since \\(N_1=I_1+\\cdots+I_N\\), linearity of expectation gives</p>\n\\[\\mathbb E[N_1]=\\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\mathbb E[I_t]=Np_1,\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\widehat p_1]=p_1.\\]\n<p>Equal marginal probabilities suffice for this expectation calculation; independence matters for the usual variance and concentration results developed later. Our sampler assumes independent fresh trials of the same model. A drifting preparation or dependent trials may require another model.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/probability-frequency.svg\" alt=\"Exact model probabilities 0.75 and 0.25 are compared with illustrative counts 72 and 28 in a batch of 100 trials.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.3.</strong> The model and one possible observed batch answer different questions. Counts 72 and 28 are an illustration, not a reported experiment or a guaranteed result.</figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"probability\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-frequency\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"conditioning\"><h2>4. When information arrives, normalize again</h2>\n<p>Suppose we learn that the weighted die's result is even. Which outcomes survive, and what should their probabilities add up to now? The surviving faces are 2, 4 and 6, in the ratio 2:3:3. Their original total probability is \\(8/12\\). Divide each surviving probability by that total:</p>\n\\[\\Pr(X=j\\mid E)=\\frac{\\Pr(\\{X=j\\}\\cap E)}{\\Pr(E)}=\\begin{cases}p_j/\\Pr(E),&j\\in E,\\\\0,&j\\notin E,\\end{cases}\\qquad \\Pr(E)>0.\\]\n<p>Our conditional distribution in full face order is therefore</p>\n\\[p_{\\mid E}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1/4\\\\0\\\\3/8\\\\0\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Check the result: \\(1/4+3/8+3/8=1\\). We have restricted the possibilities and rescaled the remaining weights. If we had only deleted the odd outcomes, the entries would still sum to 2/3, not one.</p>\n<h3>Two variables: information about one changes the description of the other</h3>\n<p>We will need this reasoning when we discuss two qubits, so let us first work a completely classical example. Let \\(X,Y\\) each take labels 0 and 1. The table contains <em>joint probabilities</em> \\(\\Pr(X=x,Y=y)\\), with rows indexed by X and columns by Y.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><caption>A joint classical distribution; all four cells sum to one</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">X label</th><th scope=\"col\">Y=0</th><th scope=\"col\">Y=1</th><th scope=\"col\">Row total P(X)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">X=0</th><td>1/12</td><td>1/4</td><td>1/3</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">X=1</th><td>1/3</td><td>1/3</td><td>2/3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>First ignore X. Summing down each column gives \\(\\Pr(Y=0)=5/12\\) and \\(\\Pr(Y=1)=7/12\\). Now suppose we learn that X=0. We retain only the first row. Its total is \\(1/12+1/4=1/3\\), so</p>\n\\[\\Pr(Y=0\\mid X=0)=\\frac{1/12}{1/3}=\\frac14,\\qquad \\Pr(Y=1\\mid X=0)=\\frac{1/4}{1/3}=\\frac34.\\]\n<p>The probabilities for Y have changed because the new information selects a particular part of the joint distribution. This example is not independent: the conditional distribution differs from the marginal distribution. If X and Y were independent, learning X would leave Y's distribution unchanged. Try both cases below.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/joint-conditioning.svg\" alt=\"Select row X=0 of the joint table and divide its entries by its total probability 1/3 to obtain conditional Y probabilities 1/4 and 3/4.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.4.</strong> Restrict the joint table to the observed row, then normalize. The denominator is the selected event’s probability.</figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"conditioning\"></div>\n<p>What if the selected event has probability zero? This elementary conditional formula is undefined. Dividing by zero cannot create a conditional distribution; an observed event excluded by our model calls for reconsidering that model.</p>\n<p>The table above contains probabilities, so we divide by an event probability. Later we will group quantum <em>amplitudes</em> by a measured label and divide the surviving amplitude vector by the square root of the outcome probability. We will derive that step from normalization rather than memorize a change of denominator.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Keep the envelope's limitation in view.</strong><p>Classical conditioning updates our information. It does not by itself describe a physical disturbance or demonstrate quantum coherence. The analogy helps us understand restricted possibilities and normalization; quantum measurement will need its own precise rules.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Work it through yourself</h2>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>The recorded result.</strong> With pass ↦ 0 and fail ↦ 1, what does \\((3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\) mean before opening the envelope? What is our updated column after observing fail?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The entries describe our probabilities for the two recorded labels. Observing fail gives the point mass \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). This is an information update, not a claim that opening the envelope changed the printed result or that the letter was a coherent quantum state.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Validity and an event.</strong> Is \\((0.1,0.4,0.5)^{\\mathsf T}\\) a valid distribution on (A,B,C)? Find the probability of not B.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Check nonnegativity and the total before adding the probabilities of the relevant outcomes.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The entries are nonnegative and sum to one. Not B contains A and C, giving \\(0.1+0.5=0.6\\), equivalently \\(1-0.4\\).</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Count or model?</strong> A fixed model has \\(p_1=0.3\\). In 200 independent trials we observe 68 ones. Calculate the expected count and observed frequency. Does this prove that the model is wrong?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The expectation is \\(200(0.3)=60\\); the frequency is \\(68/200=0.34\\). This finite observation alone does not establish an exact probability or prove the model false. Assessing a discrepancy requires an uncertainty analysis under the sampling assumptions. If conditions changed, we should also reconsider whether the fixed model is appropriate.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Condition the die.</strong> Condition on faces \\(A=\\{1,2,3\\}\\). Find \\(\\Pr(X=2\\mid A)\\). What happens for the empty event?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>\\(\\Pr(A)=4/12\\), so \\(\\Pr(X=2\\mid A)=(2/12)/(4/12)=1/2\\). The empty event has probability zero and the elementary conditional formula is undefined.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Return to the joint table.</strong> Given X=1, calculate the conditional distribution of Y. Compare it with Y's marginal distribution.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Select the X=1 row and divide by its row total, not by the total of the whole table.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The selected row is \\((1/3,1/3)\\) with total 2/3. Its normalized distribution is \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\), different from the marginal \\((5/12,7/12)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Learning X gives information about Y in this model.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we added?</strong><p>A column can describe certainty, uncertainty or random preparation, provided its meaning is stated. We can calculate events, compare a model with finite samples, and update a distribution when information arrives. Next we ask how to describe an operation acting on such a column.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-operations", "number": "2.3", "title": "Deterministic and stochastic operations", "part": "Part A · Chapter 2", "objectives": ["Build a matrix from its response to definite inputs.", "Derive the distribution update p′=Mp and prove normalization preservation.", "Distinguish stochastic validity, invertibility, and allowed reversal."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/3-operations.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "operations-asymmetric", "question": "An operation keeps input 0 unchanged and sends input 1 to 0 or 1 equally. In output order (0,1), which column represents input 1?", "options": ["(1/2,1/2)ᵀ", "(1,0)ᵀ", "(3/4,1/4)ᵀ"], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. Column 1 is the output distribution given input 1. Column 0 is (1,0)ᵀ.", "That is the output column for input 0, which the rule keeps unchanged.", "Those are the weights of a separate uncertain input preparation used in the lesson. They do not define this operation’s response to definite input 1."]}, {"id": "operations-inverse", "question": "Reset-to-zero preserves total probability. Must it therefore be reversible for every input?", "options": ["Yes: preservation of probability guarantees an inverse.", "No: distinct inputs are mapped to the same output.", "Only if the input distribution is uniform."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Probability preservation is weaker than reversibility. Reset is a counterexample.", "Correct. The common output cannot identify which of the distinct inputs occurred.", "A chosen input distribution does not create an inverse that recovers every input."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "prerequisites": "Lessons 2.1–2.2: basis columns, probability distributions and conditional probabilities.", "chapter_id": "classical-information", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">We can now describe a definite bit and uncertainty about a bit. What happens when we apply an operation? Let us begin by writing down what it does to each possible input. The matrix will follow from that rule.</p>\n<section id=\"deterministic\"><h2>1. Deterministic operations as functions</h2>\n<p>A deterministic operation on a finite state set is a function \\(f:\\Sigma\\to\\Sigma\\): for every definite input label, it specifies one definite output label. “Deterministic” describes the operation's response given its input. It does not require the input to be known to us.</p>\n<p>How many different deterministic rules can we define for one input bit and one output bit? Try listing them before reading the table. There are two choices for \\(f(0)\\) and, independently, two choices for \\(f(1)\\), giving \\(2\\times2=4\\) functions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><caption>All deterministic one-bit functions</caption><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>f(0)</th><th>f(1)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Keep the input</td></tr><tr><td>Flip</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>Exchange 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>Output 0 for either input</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Output 1 for either input</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>We could draw four input-to-output mapping diagrams. Instead, let us use the columns we have already built. For each definite input, put its output column in the corresponding position of a matrix. In symbols,</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\begin{cases}1,&i=f(j),\\\\0,&i\\ne f(j).\\end{cases}\\]\n<p>The <strong>column index is the input</strong> and the row index is the output. Column \\(j\\) is the output basis vector produced by input \\(|j\\rangle\\). Therefore \\(M|j\\rangle=|f(j)\\rangle\\). This rule is safer than memorizing matrices without knowing which direction their indices represent.</p>\n<p>In basis order \\((0,1)\\), the four matrices are</p>\n\\[I=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Test this rule on the two basis inputs yourself. For example, both columns of \\(R_0\\) are \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\), because either input is sent to 0. Multiplying by \\(|0\\rangle\\) selects the first column; multiplying by \\(|1\\rangle\\) selects the second. We have translated a rule in words into a calculation.</p><p>Remember these four functions. Later we will meet them again in Deutsch’s problem, where the question is about a property of an unknown function. Here we first understand what each function does. The symbol \\(X\\) denotes the classical flip matrix; the same numerical matrix will later act on quantum amplitudes, under a different state model.</p></section>\n<section id=\"stochastic\"><h2>2. An operation can behave probabilistically</h2>\n<p>Now consider a different rule: if the input is 0, do nothing; if the input is 1, output 0 or 1 with equal probabilities. The input can be definite even though the operation's response is random.</p>\n<h3>Predict each output before building the matrix</h3>\n<p>On input 0, the output distribution is \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). On input 1, it is \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Put these in input order (0,1):</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Try applying A to both basis columns. The results should reproduce the rule we started with:</p>\n\\[A|0\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad A|1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2\\\\1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is why reading a matrix column by column is useful. The first column says what happens given input 0; the second says what happens given input 1. We have not inferred an input probability from an output.</p>\n<p>For a general random operation, write</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j).\\]\n<p>For each fixed input j, its possible outputs form a complete probability distribution. Consequently,</p>\n\\[M_{ij}\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_iM_{ij}=1\\quad\\text{for every column }j.\\]\n<p>This defines a <strong>column-stochastic matrix</strong>. The rows of A sum to 3/2 and 1/2, which is fine: the columns are the input-conditioned distributions. Texts using row vectors may use a row-stochastic convention. We will consistently use columns.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/book-opening/operation-columns.svg\" alt=\"The two definite inputs produce columns (1,0) and (1/2,1/2), which form the asymmetric operation matrix.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.5.</strong> Each definite input selects an output distribution. These distributions become the matrix columns.</figcaption></figure>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-asymmetric\"></div>\n<h3>Build the random rule from simpler actions</h3>\n<p>We can understand the same operation another way. Choose, with equal probabilities, between doing nothing and resetting to 0. If the input is 0, either choice leaves it 0. If the input is 1, identity leaves it 1 and reset sends it to 0. Thus</p>\n\\[A=\\frac12I+\\frac12R_0=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The weights describe a random choice between two deterministic actions. We add the weighted matrices because the alternative actions are selected with those probabilities; this is different from performing one after the other.</p>\n<h3>Compare with a symmetric random flip</h3>\n<p>Suppose instead that we flip either input with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\) and keep it with probability \\(1-\\varepsilon\\), where \\(0\\le\\varepsilon\\le1\\). Then</p>\n\\[B_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X=\\begin{pmatrix}1-\\varepsilon&\\varepsilon\\\\\\varepsilon&1-\\varepsilon\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This rule treats the two inputs symmetrically; A does not. At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) we have identity; at 1 a definite flip; at 1/2 either definite input produces a uniform output. Compare A and \\(B_{1/2}\\) on input 0 to see why they are different operations.</p></section>\n<section id=\"evolution\"><h2>3. What if we do not know the input?</h2>\n<p>So far we tested definite inputs. Now suppose the preparation is described by p. Should we apply the matrix only to the most likely label? No: each possible input contributes to the output, weighted by its probability.</p>\n<p>Let \\(p_j\\) be the probability of input j. Output i can be reached through any input j. The probability for each route is the input probability times the conditional transition probability. Summing mutually exclusive input routes gives the law of total probability:</p>\n\\[p'_i=\\sum_j\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j)\\Pr(\\text{input}=j)=\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j.\\]\n<p>This is precisely the ith entry of a matrix–vector product. Therefore,</p>\n\\[p'=Mp,\\qquad \\begin{pmatrix}p'_0\\\\p'_1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}M_{00}p_0+M_{01}p_1\\\\M_{10}p_0+M_{11}p_1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<h3>Work the lecture's asymmetric operation on an uncertain input</h3>\n<p>Use the familiar input \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Predict the two output probabilities, then calculate:</p>\n\\[Ap=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4+(1/2)(1/4)\\\\(1/2)(1/4)\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}7/8\\\\1/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Why 7/8 for output 0? All the probability already on input 0 stays there, and half the probability on input 1 also arrives at 0. For output 1, only the input-1 route contributes, and only half of it survives. The multiplication records those routes.</p>\n<p>As a comparison, \\(B_{1/4}\\) acting on the same p gives</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}p=\\begin{pmatrix}9/16+1/16\\\\3/16+3/16\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5/8\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The different results follow from different transition rules, not a change in the input. Check both rules in the experiment after making your own prediction.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"operations\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"normalization\"><h2>4. Why the output is still a probability distribution</h2>\n<p>We should verify that the update rule respects the definition of a distribution. Each output entry is a sum of products of nonnegative numbers, so \\(p'_i\\ge0\\). For the total probability, interchange the finite sums:</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i p'_i&=\\sum_i\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j\\left(\\sum_iM_{ij}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The third line uses the fact that each column of \\(M\\) sums to one. Thus every valid column-stochastic matrix maps every valid input distribution to another valid distribution. This proof explains why the column condition is the right one; checking a few numerical inputs alone would not establish the general statement.</p>\n<p>A deterministic function is a special stochastic operation: each column has one 1 and all other entries 0. It still acts on uncertain input distributions by the same rule. There are therefore two distinct sources of uncertainty: uncertainty in the input preparation and randomness in the operation. They can occur separately or together.</p></section>\n<section id=\"reversibility\"><h2>5. Preserving probability does not imply reversibility</h2>\n<p>If both inputs become 0, can we recover the input from that output? This question separates probability preservation from reversibility. The flip can be undone: applying it twice restores every input, so \\(X^{-1}=X\\). Identity is its own inverse too. Reset-to-zero sends both input labels to the same output, so the output cannot tell us which input occurred.</p>\n<p>There cannot be an inverse operation recovering every reset input, because it would have to send the same vector \\(|0\\rangle\\) to two different vectors. The identical columns of \\(R_0\\) make that loss of input information visible. Reset nevertheless preserves total probability perfectly.</p>\n<p>An invertible numerical matrix need not have an inverse that is a physical stochastic operation. For example,</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/2&-1/2\\\\-1/2&3/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is an algebraic inverse, but it contains negative entries. It cannot act on every probability distribution as a stochastic process. Deterministic bijections, represented by permutation matrices, do have stochastic inverses. This distinction prepares us for the stronger restrictions on closed-system quantum operations later.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-inverse\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Work it through yourself</h2><p>Start by specifying the input-conditioned columns. Then calculate and explain the result; an answer without the interpretation leaves half the task unfinished.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>A random choice or a sequence?</strong> Explain why choosing identity or reset-to-zero with equal probabilities gives A, but applying identity and then reset-to-zero does not.<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>A random choice gives \\((I+R_0)/2=A\\), with input 1 producing a half/half distribution. A sequence gives \\(R_0I=R_0\\), which always outputs 0. Addition with probabilistic weights and temporal composition describe different experiments.</p></details></li><li><strong>Build a matrix from words.</strong> An operation leaves 0 unchanged. Given 1, it outputs 0 with probability 1/3 and 1 with probability 2/3. Construct its matrix and apply it to \\(p=(1/4,3/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\).\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Fill the first column from input 0 and the second from input 1. Then multiply by the input probabilities.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The matrix is \\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/3\\\\0&2/3\\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore \\(Mp=(1/4+(1/3)(3/4),\\ (2/3)(3/4))^{\\mathsf T}=(1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Both columns sum to one, and the result is normalized.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Check the convention.</strong> Is \\(C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\) a valid operation on our column probability vectors?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. The column sums are 3/2 and 1/2. For example, \\(C|0\\rangle=(1,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is not a normalized distribution. Its rows summing to one does not make it valid under our column convention.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Reversal.</strong> Explain why reset cannot be reversed for every unknown input. Is the algebraic inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) a stochastic matrix?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Reset maps distinct inputs to the same output, so no function of that output can recover both inputs correctly. The stated inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) reverses the matrix algebra but has negative entries, so it is not a stochastic operation on arbitrary distributions.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we learned about an operation?</strong><p>Begin with its rule on each definite input. Those output columns build the matrix, and total probability gives \\(p'=Mp\\). Check normalization, then ask separately whether information about the input can be recovered. Next we connect operations in sequence.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-composition", "number": "2.4", "title": "Composing operations", "part": "Part A · Chapter 2", "objectives": ["Translate a temporal sequence into the correct matrix product.", "Show a noncommuting pair and distinguish it from associativity.", "Prove that a compatible product of stochastic matrices is stochastic."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/4-composition.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "composition-order", "question": "A acts first, then B, on a column probability vector p. Which expression describes the result?", "options": ["ABp", "BAp", "A+B+p"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["ABp applies B to p first; it reverses the stated sequence.", "Correct. The intermediate state is Ap, so the second action is B(Ap).", "A temporal composition is matrix multiplication, not addition."]}, {"id": "composition-commute", "question": "Identity and flip agree on the uniform bit distribution. Are they therefore the same operation?", "options": ["Yes: one matching output proves equality.", "No: they disagree on a definite basis input.", "They agree on every other input too."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["One input is not enough to establish equality of linear maps.", "Correct. On |0⟩, identity outputs |0⟩ while flip outputs |1⟩.", "They agree on the uniform vector, but not on the basis inputs."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "prerequisites": "Lesson 2.3: operation columns and p′=Mp. The matrix-multiplication refresher below supplies the required arithmetic.", "chapter_id": "classical-information", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Suppose we connect two small devices: the first changes a bit, and the second acts on its output. Can we replace the pair by one operation? Let us follow the intermediate state first, then see why a matrix product describes the whole process.</p>\n<section id=\"refresher\"><h2>1. A short matrix-multiplication refresher</h2>\n<p>We already have state columns and operation matrices. Before combining devices, let us check the multiplication itself. A matrix–vector product takes one row at a time, multiplies its entries by the matching vector entries, and adds the results. For</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad v=\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\w\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Av=\\begin{pmatrix}au+bw\\\\cu+dw\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>A matrix–matrix product applies the same rule to each column of the right-hand matrix. If</p>\n\\[B=\\begin{pmatrix}e&f\\\\g&h\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>then</p>\n\\[AB=\\begin{pmatrix}ae+bg&af+bh\\\\ce+dg&cf+dh\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is not entry-by-entry multiplication. The \\((i,j)\\) entry is \\((AB)_{ij}=\\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}\\). If \\(B\\) maps input states to intermediate states and \\(A\\) maps intermediate states to output states, this sum follows every possible intermediate route.</p>\n<p>Dimensions are part of the meaning. If \\(B\\) maps a \\(d\\)-state input to an \\(r\\)-state intermediate system and \\(A\\) maps that to an \\(m\\)-state output, their dimensions are \\(r\\times d\\) and \\(m\\times r\\). The product \\(AB\\) is \\(m\\times d\\). Its inner dimensions match because the two operations share the same intermediate labels and order. Our lab uses two-state systems, but the rule is more general.</p></section>\n<section id=\"order\"><h2>2. Why the rightmost matrix acts first</h2>\n<p>Think first about ordinary functions. If we apply f and then g, we write \\(g(f(x))\\): f acts on the input before g. For our devices, start with a column \\(p\\). Apply \\(A\\), obtaining the intermediate state \\(p^{(1)}=Ap\\). The second device B receives that result:</p>\n\\[p^{(2)}=Bp^{(1)}=B(Ap)=(BA)p.\\]\n<p>The last equality uses <strong>associativity</strong>: compatible matrix multiplication can be regrouped without changing its value. It does not permit us to interchange the factors. The operation closest to the input column acts first. Thus the temporal instruction “A, then B” corresponds to \\(BA\\), not \\(AB\\).</p>\n<p>For an ordered sequence \\(M_1,M_2,\\ldots,M_n\\),</p>\n\\[p^{(n)}=M_nM_{n-1}\\cdots M_2M_1p.\\]\n<p>There is no conflict between a process diagram read left to right and a product acting right to left. They are two notations for the same sequence. Write an intermediate state whenever the shorthand becomes confusing.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-order\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"counterexample\"><h2>3. Worked example: reset and flip do not commute</h2>\n<p>Use the reset-to-zero and flip matrices</p>\n\\[R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Before looking at a product, predict each stage. If we reset a bit to 0 and then flip it, what must the final label be? If we reverse the devices, what changes? Start with any normalized bit distribution \\(p=(p_0,p_1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Reset first:</p>\n\\[R_0p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_0+p_1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Flipping that result gives \\(X(R_0p)=(0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\): the output is certainly 1. The combined matrix confirms this:</p>\n\\[XR_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=R_1.\\]\n<p>Reverse the physical order. Flipping the input first exchanges its probabilities, but resetting afterwards always gives 0:</p>\n\\[R_0(Xp)=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad R_0X=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}=R_0.\\]\n<p>Consequently \\(XR_0\\ne R_0X\\). These operations <strong>do not commute</strong>. The difference is not a rounding error or a matter of which input was most probable: the two output distributions are different for every normalized input.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/composition-order.svg\" alt=\"Reset to zero followed by flip always outputs one and has product X R0. Flip followed by reset always outputs zero and has product R0 X.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.6.</strong> Temporal order and matrix order describe the same calculation. Associativity allows regrouping; it does not allow exchanging reset and flip.</figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"composition\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"commuting\"><h2>4. Some operations do commute</h2>\n<p>“Matrix multiplication is not commutative” means we cannot assume \\(AB=BA\\) for arbitrary matrices. It does not mean no pair ever commutes. Identity commutes with every compatible square matrix. Any matrix commutes with itself. Less trivially, a random-flip matrix \\(B_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X\\) commutes with \\(X\\):</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}XB_\\varepsilon&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I,\\\\B_\\varepsilon X&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>We used \\(X^2=I\\), which simply says that two definite bit flips undo one another. Equality of outputs for one chosen input, however, is not enough to prove equality of two operation matrices. They might agree on that input and disagree on another. Equality as linear maps can be checked on an entire basis, since every vector is a linear combination of basis vectors.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-commute\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"closure\"><h2>5. A composition is still a valid stochastic operation</h2>\n<p>Let \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be compatible column-stochastic matrices. The combined operation is \\(C=BA\\), whose entries are</p>\n\\[C_{ij}=\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}.\\]\n<p>Every entry is nonnegative because each term is a product of nonnegative entries. For a fixed input column \\(j\\),</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i C_{ij}&=\\sum_i\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}\\left(\\sum_iB_{ik}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The penultimate equality uses the column sum of \\(B\\); the final equality uses the column sum of \\(A\\). Therefore \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic. By applying this argument repeatedly, any finite sequence of valid stochastic operations remains a valid stochastic operation.</p>\n<p>The probabilistic interpretation is also useful. The index \\(k\\) ranges over the intermediate state. The first operation sends input \\(j\\) to \\(k\\) with probability \\(A_{kj}\\), and the second sends \\(k\\) to output \\(i\\) with probability \\(B_{ik}\\). Multiplying the route probabilities and summing over intermediate labels gives the composite transition rule.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A modelling assumption behind the product</strong><p>The same second-operation rule \\(B\\) must apply given the intermediate state. If the second device also depends on hidden history, shared randomness, or a changing environment not represented in that state, the state description may need to be enlarged. A convenient matrix product does not eliminate the need for a faithful model.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Work it through yourself</h2><p>Write the input, the intermediate state and the output before simplifying the combined matrix. Then check that both calculation routes agree.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Return to the asymmetric rule.</strong> Begin with \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Apply the previous lesson’s operation A that keeps 0 and randomizes input 1 equally, then apply a definite flip X. Find the intermediate vector, final vector, and combined matrix.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>The intermediate result is Ap. The second operation gives X(Ap), so the combined matrix is XA.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The intermediate result is \\((7/8,1/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Flipping gives \\((1/8,7/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Multiplication gives \\(XA=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1/2\\\\1&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\), which produces the same final vector when applied directly to p. Reversing the devices instead gives \\(A(Xp)=(5/8,3/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\), so this pair also demonstrates why temporal order matters.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>One input is not a proof.</strong> Identity and flip both leave the uniform distribution \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) unchanged. Are they the same operation?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. On input \\(|0\\rangle\\), identity outputs \\(|0\\rangle\\) while flip outputs \\(|1\\rangle\\). Agreement on one vector is weaker than agreement as operators. Checking their action on both bit basis vectors would distinguish them.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Three operations.</strong> A process applies \\(A\\), then \\(B\\), then \\(C\\). Write the combined matrix. If all are column-stochastic, explain why their product is valid.\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The combined matrix is \\(CBA\\). The closure proof shows \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic, and applying it again shows \\(C(BA)\\) is column-stochastic. Associativity allows the grouping; no factor has been interchanged.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>What history must be modelled?</strong> A second device behaves differently depending on the original input, even when the same intermediate bit reaches it. Can a single matrix conditioned only on the intermediate bit describe both cases?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Not in general. If the same represented intermediate state leads to different conditional output rules depending on omitted history, that bit alone is not a sufficient state description for the proposed composition model. Include the relevant history/environment label or explicitly specify a different operation for each context.</p></details></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"readiness\"><h2>7. Chapter checkpoint and the road to quantum computing</h2>\n<p>You now have a complete classical calculation pattern: declare the state labels and order; write a probability vector; represent conditional transitions by stochastic columns; compose the matrices in temporal order; interpret the resulting distribution. You should be able to explain each step without relying on a simulation.</p>\n<ul><li>Represent a definite state and an uncertain description in the same declared basis.</li><li>Distinguish a probability, an empirical frequency, and a conditional probability.</li><li>Build an operation from its response to every definite input.</li><li>Derive \\(p'=Mp\\) and explain why normalization is preserved.</li><li>Track intermediate states and show an explicit noncommuting example.</li></ul>\n<p>Keep the four one-bit functions and the rule “write the intermediate state” in mind. They will return when we work through Deutsch’s algorithm. Knowing what the classical functions do will help us ask a different question about an unknown function.</p><p>Next we develop the complex-number and inner-product language needed for quantum states. Matrix composition will still matter, but the allowed states, operations and measurement rules will change. We will introduce the mathematics where it helps us make that change precisely, while keeping the route toward the classical ML bridge and QML visible.</p>\n</section>\n"}, {"id": "math-complex", "number": "3.1", "title": "Complex numbers, magnitude and phase", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Compute conjugates, products and squared magnitudes.", "Connect rectangular and polar forms without confusing the complex plane with a qubit picture."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/1.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-complex-check-1", "question": "For z=(1+2i)/3, which quantity is 5/9?", "options": ["z squared", "The squared magnitude of z", "The imaginary part of z"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Squaring gives (−3+4i)/9.", "Correct. Multiply z by its conjugate.", "The imaginary part is 2/3."]}, {"id": "math-complex-check-2", "question": "What happens to the phase at z=0?", "options": ["It must be π/2.", "It uniquely equals zero.", "It is undefined; any angle in the polar formula gives the same zero."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["There is no direction from the origin to itself.", "A display may choose zero by convention, but that is not a unique phase.", "Correct. Magnitude zero removes the angular information."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">In Chapter 2, entries in a probability column were nonnegative real numbers. Quantum amplitudes need a richer number system. Before assigning those amplitudes any physical meaning, let us become comfortable calculating with complex numbers.</p>\n<section id=\"plane\"><h2>1. A number with two real coordinates</h2><p>A complex number has the form \\(z=a+bi\\), where a and b are real and \\(i^2=-1\\). Its real part is a, and its imaginary part is b, not bi. Addition follows the coordinates: \\((a+bi)+(c+di)=(a+c)+(b+d)i\\). Multiplication uses ordinary expansion, followed by replacing \\(i^2\\) with −1:</p>\n\\[(a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i.\\]\n<p>For example, \\((1+2i)(2-i)=2-i+4i-2i^2=4+3i\\). The minus sign in \\(i^2=-1\\) is essential. A complex number can be drawn as a point (a,b), or an arrow from the origin, on the complex plane. The horizontal axis records the real part and the vertical axis the imaginary part. This plane describes one complex number; it is not a Bloch sphere or the physical position of a qubit.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/complex-plane.svg\" alt=\"The point 1+2i and its conjugate 1−2i are reflections across the real axis, with equal magnitude.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.1.</strong> The complex plane separates magnitude, phase and conjugation.</figcaption></figure>\n</section><section id=\"conjugate\"><h2>2. Conjugation, magnitude and an important distinction</h2><p>The conjugate \\(z^*=\\overline z=a-bi\\) reflects the point across the real axis. Multiplying by the conjugate gives a nonnegative real number:</p>\n\\[z^*z=(a-bi)(a+bi)=a^2+b^2,\\qquad |z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.\\]\n<p>The cross terms cancel and the remaining term \\(-b^2i^2\\) becomes \\(+b^2\\). This explains why we use the squared magnitude rather than the ordinary square when constructing probabilities. In particular,</p>\n\\[z=\\frac{1+2i}{3}:\\quad z^*=\\frac{1-2i}{3},\\quad |z|^2=\\frac59,\\quad z^2=\\frac{-3+4i}{9}.\\]\n<p>These last two expressions answer different questions. The squared magnitude is 5/9; the ordinary square still has an imaginary component. A single amplitude with magnitude below one is not, by itself, a complete normalized quantum state: we must account for all the state's amplitudes.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-complex-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"phase\"><h2>3. Magnitude and phase</h2><p>A nonzero point can also be described by distance r from the origin and angle φ from the positive real axis:</p>\n\\[z=r(\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi)=re^{i\\phi},\\quad r=|z|,\\quad a=r\\cos\\phi,\\quad b=r\\sin\\phi.\\]\n<p>The identity \\(e^{i\\phi}=\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi\\) is Euler’s formula. We use radians in equations: π radians is 180°. Phase is defined modulo \\(2\\pi\\), so φ and \\(\\phi+2\\pi\\) describe the same number. At z=0, the magnitude is zero and there is no unique phase.</p><p>Multiplying by \\(e^{i\\gamma}\\) rotates a point through γ without changing its magnitude. For example, \\(i=e^{i\\pi/2}\\), so multiplying 1+2i by i gives −2+i, a quarter-turn. Conjugation is a reflection, whereas multiplication by i is a rotation. They are different operations.</p><p>Quantum calculations use such phase factors when amplitudes are combined. Two amplitudes can have the same magnitude but interfere differently because their phases differ. We will develop that application after learning how to combine vector entries.</p><div data-math-lab=\"complex\"></div><div data-quiz=\"math-complex-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Calculate before simplifying.</strong> Find \\((2-i)(1+3i)\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Expand the product before replacing i² by −1.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Expansion gives \\(2+6i-i-3i^2=5+5i\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Square or squared magnitude?</strong> For \\(z=1+3i\\), find \\(z^*, |z|^2, z^2\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Calculate the ordinary square and the conjugate product separately.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>They are \\(1-3i\\), 10 and \\(-8+6i\\), respectively. Only the squared magnitude is necessarily nonnegative real.</p></details></li><li><strong>Rotate a point.</strong> Multiply \\(-1+i\\) by i. Does its magnitude change?<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Distribute i across both terms, then compare squared magnitudes.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The result is \\(-1-i\\). Both magnitudes are \\(\\sqrt2\\); the phase increases by π/2 modulo 2π.</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-dot-norm", "number": "3.2", "title": "Dot products, norms and projections", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Calculate real dot products and connect their signs to angles.", "Normalize nonzero vectors and distinguish scalar from vector projections."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/2.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-dot-norm-check-1", "question": "For nonzero u, when does u·v equal the signed projection length of v along u?", "options": ["Always", "When u has unit length", "Only when v has unit length"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["For arbitrary u, divide by its length to get the signed component.", "Correct. The direction vector must be normalized.", "Normalizing v does not remove the scale of u."]}, {"id": "math-dot-norm-check-2", "question": "Can (0,0) be normalized to a unit vector by dividing by its norm?", "options": ["Yes: it is already normalized.", "Yes: divide each entry by 1.", "No: its norm is zero."], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["Its length is zero, not one.", "That leaves a zero vector.", "Correct. There is no direction to preserve and division by zero is undefined."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Vectors let us keep several quantities together. The dot product tells us how two real vectors align, while the norm tells us their length. We will calculate these quantities before extending them to complex entries.</p>\n<section id=\"dot\"><h2>1. Multiply matching entries and add</h2><p>For two real column vectors with the same dimension, define</p>\n\\[u\\cdot v=u^{\\mathsf T}v=\\sum_{j=1}^{d}u_jv_j.\\]\n<p>The transpose turns u into a row; multiplying a 1×d row by a d×1 column gives one scalar. With \\(u=(3,4)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\(v=(2,-1)^{\\mathsf T}\\), the dot product is \\(3(2)+4(-1)=2\\). It is neither a new vector nor the entrywise product (6,−4). Real dot products are symmetric and linear in each argument.</p><p>In a real Euclidean space the same quantity has a geometric meaning:</p>\n\\[u\\cdot v=\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta.\\]\n<p>For nonzero vectors, an acute angle gives a positive dot product, a right angle gives zero and an obtuse angle gives a negative value. If either vector is zero the product is zero, but an angle involving that zero vector is not defined. The coordinate calculation remains valid in higher dimensions even when a drawing becomes difficult.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/dot-projection.svg\" alt=\"A vector v is decomposed into its projection along unit direction u and a perpendicular residual.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.2.</strong> The signed component along a unit direction determines the projection vector.</figcaption></figure>\n</section><section id=\"norm\"><h2>2. Length and normalization</h2>\n\\[\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{v\\cdot v}=\\sqrt{\\sum_j v_j^2}.\\]\n<p>Our vector (3,4) has length 5 by the Pythagorean theorem. To retain its direction and make its length one, divide every entry by the same positive length:</p>\n\\[\\widehat v=\\frac{v}{\\|v\\|}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/5\\\\4/5\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\|\\widehat v\\|^2=\\frac9{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1.\\]\n<p>This is normalization. A zero vector cannot be normalized, because division by its norm would divide by zero. Multiplying by a negative real number reverses direction; dividing by the positive norm does not. Unit length alone does not make a vector a quantum state of a specified system—we must also state its space and interpretation.</p><p>Classical probability normalization in Chapter 2 required the entries themselves to sum to one. Euclidean normalization instead requires the squared entries to sum to one for real vectors. Do not interchange those rules: (3/7,4/7) is a probability column but not a unit Euclidean vector.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-dot-norm-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"projection\"><h2>3. Scalar and vector projections</h2><p>If \\(\\widehat u=u/\\|u\\|\\) is a unit direction, the signed scalar component of v along it is \\(\\widehat u\\cdot v\\). The vector projection is that scalar times the direction:</p>\n\\[\\operatorname{proj}_u(v)=\\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}u\\quad (u\\ne0).\\]\n<p>For u=(1,0) and v=(3,4), the scalar component is 3 and the projected vector is (3,0). The residual (0,4) is perpendicular to u. With u=(2,0), the dot product becomes 6, but the projected vector remains (3,0). Thus a dot product with a non-unit vector is not directly a projection length.</p><div data-math-lab=\"dot\"></div><p><strong>Takeaway.</strong> A dot product is a scalar, a norm is a nonnegative length, and a vector projection is a vector. Keeping their types distinct prevents many later errors.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-dot-norm-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Calculate alignment.</strong> Find \\((1,2)\\cdot(2,-1)\\) and interpret it.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Multiply matching entries and add.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The result is \\(2-2=0\\). The two nonzero real vectors are perpendicular.</p></details></li><li><strong>Normalize.</strong> Normalize \\((1,2,2)^{\\mathsf T}\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Find the square root of the sum of the squared entries.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The norm is 3, so divide by 3 to obtain \\((1/3,2/3,2/3)^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Use a non-unit direction.</strong> Project \\(v=(1,2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) onto \\(u=(1,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Use (u·v)/(u·u), then multiply by u.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Here \\(u\\cdot v=3\\) and \\(u\\cdot u=2\\). The projection is \\((3/2,3/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\); the residual \\((-1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) has zero dot product with u.</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-inner", "number": "3.3", "title": "Bras, kets and complex inner products", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Construct conjugate transposes and calculate inner products.", "Check normalization and orthogonality and interpret normalized overlap."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/3.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-inner-check-1", "question": "What is the squared norm of v=(1,i)?", "options": ["0", "2", "1+i"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["That is the incorrect non-conjugated self-product.", "Correct: |1|²+|i|²=2.", "Squared norm is real and nonnegative."]}, {"id": "math-inner-check-2", "question": "If an inner product equals i/2, what is its squared magnitude?", "options": ["−1/4", "1/2", "1/4"], "answer": 2, "feedback": ["The ordinary square is −1/4; probability uses squared magnitude.", "Square the magnitude 1/2.", "Correct: (−i/2)(i/2)=1/4."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">The real dot product uses a transpose. Complex entries require a conjugate transpose. This small change makes squared length nonnegative and gives bra–ket notation its computational meaning.</p>\n<section id=\"bra\"><h2>1. From a ket to a bra</h2><p>A ket \\(|v\\rangle\\) is a column vector. Its associated bra is the conjugate transpose, denoted \\(\\langle v|=(|v\\rangle)^\\dagger\\). First conjugate every entry, then turn the column into a row:</p>\n\\[|v\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1+i\\\\2-i\\end{pmatrix}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\langle v|=\\begin{pmatrix}1-i&amp;2+i\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Conjugation and transposition commute, so reversing those two steps gives the same result. For real entries conjugation changes nothing. This is why the basis bras in Chapter 2 looked like ordinary transposes; their entries were all real.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/bra-ket.svg\" alt=\"A complex column (1+i,2−i) becomes (1−i,2+i) after conjugation and transposition.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.3.</strong> A bra is the conjugate transpose of its ket.</figcaption></figure>\n</section><section id=\"inner\"><h2>2. The complex inner product</h2>\n\\[\\langle u|v\\rangle=u^\\dagger v=\\sum_j u_j^*v_j,\\qquad \\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v|v\\rangle}.\\]\n<p>We use the physics convention: the first argument is conjugated. The product is linear in the second argument and conjugate-linear in the first. In particular, \\(\\langle cu|v\\rangle=c^*\\langle u|v\\rangle\\), while \\(\\langle u|cv\\rangle=c\\langle u|v\\rangle\\). Swapping arguments gives \\(\\langle v|u\\rangle=\\langle u|v\\rangle^*\\).</p><p>Why is conjugation necessary? Consider v=(1,i). Without conjugation the self-product would be \\(1+i^2=0\\), although v is nonzero. With conjugation it is \\(1+(-i)i=2\\). More generally, \\(\\langle v|v\\rangle=\\sum_j|v_j|^2\\), which is positive for every nonzero v.</p><h3>Worked calculation</h3>\n\\[|u\\rangle=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\i\\end{pmatrix},\\quad |v\\rangle=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}i\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\langle u|v\\rangle=\\frac{i-i}{2}=0.\\]\n<p>Both vectors have unit norm. Their inner product is zero, so they are orthogonal. If we had transposed u without conjugating it, the incorrect answer would be i. The result is an error in the operation, not a different physical prediction.</p><div data-math-lab=\"inner\"></div><div data-quiz=\"math-inner-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"overlap\"><h2>3. Orthogonality, coefficients and overlap</h2><p>An orthonormal basis has unit vectors satisfying \\(\\langle e_j|e_k\\rangle=\\delta_{jk}\\): one for matching indices, zero otherwise. If \\(|v\\rangle=\\sum_k v_k|e_k\\rangle\\), multiplying by \\(\\langle e_j|\\) picks out the coefficient \\(v_j\\). That is why \\(\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle\\) returns the amplitude on basis state 0.</p><p>For normalized vectors, the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality ensures \\(|\\langle u|v\\rangle|\\le1\\). In a rank-one quantum projection onto normalized u, the probability for that outcome is \\(|\\langle u|v\\rangle|^2\\). This interpretation assumes a normalized input v and the specified measurement; an arbitrary raw dot product is not automatically a probability.</p><p>For example, \\(\\langle0|+\\rangle=1/\\sqrt2\\), so the probability of outcome 0 in a computational-basis measurement of plus is 1/2. Orthogonal pure states can be perfectly distinguished by an appropriate ideal measurement; states with nonzero overlap cannot always be distinguished perfectly from one copy.</p><p>A real-plane perpendicular-arrow picture motivates orthogonality, but a general complex inner product need not be real. We use the algebraic condition of zero inner product, rather than forcing complex vectors into an ordinary two-dimensional drawing.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-inner-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Construct a bra.</strong> Find the bra of \\((2i,1-i)^{\\mathsf T}\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Conjugate both entries, then write a row.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It is the row \\((-2i,1+i)\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Normalize complex entries.</strong> Find positive N such that \\(N(1-i,2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) has unit norm.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Add squared magnitudes before finding the normalization factor.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The unscaled squared norm is \\(|1-i|^2+|2|^2=2+4=6\\), so \\(N=1/\\sqrt6\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Orthogonality and phase.</strong> If \\(\\langle u|v\\rangle=0\\), does replacing v by \\(e^{i\\phi}v\\) preserve orthogonality?<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>The inner product is linear in its second argument.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Yes: linearity in the second argument gives \\(\\langle u|e^{i\\phi}v\\rangle=e^{i\\phi}0=0\\).</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-outer", "number": "3.4", "title": "Outer products and projectors", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Distinguish scalar inner products from matrix outer products.", "Construct rank-one projectors and separate projection, probability and normalization."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/4.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-outer-check-1", "question": "Which expression is a matrix?", "options": ["⟨u|v⟩", "|u⟩⟨v|", "The norm of u"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["An inner product is a scalar.", "Correct: an m×1 column times a 1×n row is an m×n matrix.", "A norm is a nonnegative scalar."]}, {"id": "math-outer-check-2", "question": "A projected measurement branch has squared norm p>0. How do we normalize it?", "options": ["Divide by p", "Divide by √p", "Multiply by p"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["That would generally leave squared norm 1/p.", "Correct. Its norm, rather than squared norm, is √p.", "That shrinks the vector instead of making its norm one."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Changing the order of a row and column changes the kind of answer. A bra times a ket gives a scalar. A ket times a bra gives a matrix. That matrix can act on another vector.</p>\n<section id=\"outer\"><h2>1. Construct an outer product</h2>\n\\[|u\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\i\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\langle v|=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-i\\end{pmatrix},\\quad |u\\rangle\\langle v|=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-i\\\\2i&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Each column is u multiplied by the corresponding entry of the bra. The bottom-right entry is \\(i(-i)=1\\). In general, u with m entries and v with n entries give an m×n matrix with entry \\(u_jv_k^*\\). The notation \\(|u\\rangle\\langle v|\\) includes conjugation through the bra; do not conjugate its entries a second time.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/products.svg\" alt=\"A row times column gives a scalar; a column times row gives a matrix; two columns combine by tensor product into a longer column.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.4.</strong> Inner, outer and tensor products have different input shapes and output types.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When this matrix acts on w, associativity gives \\((|u\\rangle\\langle v|)|w\\rangle=|u\\rangle(\\langle v|w\\rangle)\\). The inner product on the right is one scalar, which scales u. This is a useful way to understand what a rank-one operator does.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-outer-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"projectors\"><h2>2. Projection onto a unit direction</h2><p>For a normalized vector u, define \\(P_u=|u\\rangle\\langle u|\\). Acting on v gives the component along that direction:</p>\n\\[P_u|v\\rangle=\\langle u|v\\rangle|u\\rangle,\\qquad P_u^2=|u\\rangle\\underbrace{\\langle u|u\\rangle}_{1}\\langle u|=P_u.\\]\n<p>The second identity is idempotence: projecting an already projected vector changes nothing. A projector is also Hermitian, meaning \\(P_u^\\dagger=P_u\\). These properties concern the linear projection; subsequent normalization of a measurement branch is a separate step.</p><p>Normalization of u matters. For a nonzero direction that is not unit length, the projector is \\(P_u=uu^\\dagger/(u^\\dagger u)\\). For u=(2,0), the raw outer product is diag(4,0), which is not idempotent. Dividing by 4 gives diag(1,0), the actual projector.</p>\n<div data-math-lab=\"projector\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"measurement\"><h2>3. Projection, probability and a normalized branch</h2><p>Let \\(|\\psi\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+\\sqrt3|1\\rangle)/2\\) and \\(P_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)\\). There are three distinct calculations:</p>\n\\[P_0|\\psi\\rangle=\\tfrac12|0\\rangle,\\quad p_0=\\|P_0|\\psi\\rangle\\|^2=\\tfrac14,\\quad \\frac{P_0|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_0}}=|0\\rangle.\\]\n<p>The first result is an unnormalized vector, the second a probability, and the third the state conditioned on that outcome. The last step is valid only if \\(p_0&gt;0\\). A zero-probability branch cannot be normalized. We divide amplitudes by the square root of the probability, not by the probability itself.</p><p>For an orthonormal basis the projectors satisfy \\(\\sum_jP_j=I\\). Every normalized input therefore has total outcome probability one. We develop the measurement rules more fully in Chapter 4; here we have established the algebra that makes them readable.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-outer-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Build the matrix.</strong> Compute \\(|+\\rangle\\langle+|\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Write plus as a column and its bra as a row.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It is \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\). Multiplying it by itself returns the same matrix.</p></details></li><li><strong>Keep the conjugate.</strong> Find \\(uu^\\dagger\\) for \\(u=(1,i)^{\\mathsf T}/\\sqrt2\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>The conjugate of i is −i.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The result is \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-i\\\\i&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\). It is Hermitian and idempotent.</p></details></li><li><strong>Condition correctly.</strong> For \\(\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\), project onto basis state 1 and normalize.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Separate the projected vector from its squared norm.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The projected vector is \\((0,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\), with probability 1/4. Divide it by 1/2 to obtain \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-matrices", "number": "3.5", "title": "Matrices, linear maps and adjoints", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Interpret matrix columns as transformed basis vectors.", "Track composition order and dimensions; calculate adjoints."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/5.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-matrices-check-1", "question": "A acts first, followed by B. Which matrix describes the composition?", "options": ["AB", "BA", "A+B"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["The rightmost matrix acts first on a column.", "Correct: B(Av)=(BA)v.", "Addition combines outputs, not successive operations."]}, {"id": "math-matrices-check-2", "question": "Is every Hermitian matrix unitary?", "options": ["Yes", "No; diag(1,2) is Hermitian but not unitary.", "Only when its entries are real."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Hermitian and unitary are different conditions.", "Correct. Its adjoint product is diag(1,4), not identity.", "Being real does not enforce norm preservation."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Chapter 2 used matrices to describe classical operations. We now make the linear-algebra rules explicit, allowing complex entries and keeping track of dimensions at every step.</p>\n<section id=\"action\"><h2>1. A matrix is specified by what it does to basis vectors</h2><p>An m×n matrix A maps an n-entry column to an m-entry column. If its columns are \\(c_1,\\ldots,c_n\\), then</p>\n\\[Av=\\sum_{k=1}^n v_kc_k,\\qquad A e_k=c_k.\\]\n<p>This is the same column interpretation used for classical stochastic operations. Matrix multiplication itself is not specifically classical or quantum; the restrictions on the allowed matrices and state vectors depend on the model.</p><p>For example,</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad v=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Av=3\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}-\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>A is a shear in a real-plane picture. Its first basis column stays fixed and its second tilts. The same matrix acts on complex vectors too; the real drawing shows its action on real inputs.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/matrix-map.svg\" alt=\"A linear transformation moves two basis arrows and transforms a vector by combining those transformed basis arrows.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.5.</strong> The columns specify the images of the standard basis. A real-plane shear illustrates linear action.</figcaption></figure>\n</section><section id=\"linearity\"><h2>2. Linearity and composition</h2><p>A linear map obeys \\(A(cu+dv)=cAu+dAv\\). This permits us to transform the basis components separately and add the results. A map that adds a fixed nonzero vector is not linear: it does not send the zero vector to zero.</p><p>If A acts first and B second, the result is \\(B(Av)=(BA)v\\). The rightmost matrix acts first. Let \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), \\(B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\) and v=(1,0). Applying A then B gives (0,2); applying B then A gives (0,1). Thus the order can change the answer.</p><p>Check dimensions before multiplying. An m×n A followed by a k×m B yields a k×n BA. A matrix with two columns cannot act directly on a three-entry vector. A result with incompatible dimensions indicates a malformed calculation rather than an exotic quantum effect.</p><div data-math-lab=\"matrix\"></div><div data-quiz=\"math-matrices-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"adjoint\"><h2>3. Transpose, adjoint and Hermitian matrices</h2><p>The transpose swaps rows with columns. The adjoint, or conjugate transpose, also conjugates every entry:</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;i\\\\2-i&amp;3\\end{pmatrix},\\quad A^\\dagger=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2+i\\\\-i&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>For an m×n matrix, its adjoint has shape n×m. A key identity is \\((BA)^\\dagger=A^\\dagger B^\\dagger\\): taking an adjoint reverses the order. One way to remember it is to follow the dimensions in the reversed maps.</p><p>A square matrix satisfying \\(A^\\dagger=A\\) is <strong>Hermitian</strong>. Its diagonal entries are real and off-diagonal entries occur in conjugate pairs. The matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;i\\\\-i&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}\\) is Hermitian. This is a different condition from unitarity. Projectors are Hermitian, yet usually are not invertible. Later, Hermitian operators will describe observables; we do not need a full eigenvalue theory to perform the calculations here.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-matrices-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Read the columns.</strong> If \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\), find \\(Ae_0,Ae_1\\) in order (0,1).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>A basis input selects the corresponding matrix column.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>They are the first and second columns: \\((2,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\((-1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Check the dimensions.</strong> A has shape 3×2 and B has shape 4×3. Which composition is defined?<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Compare the inner dimensions of each proposed product.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>BA is 4×2. AB is not defined because its inner dimensions would be 2 and 4.</p></details></li><li><strong>Conjugate and transpose.</strong> Find the adjoint of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2i\\\\-i&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Both conjugation and exchange of row/column positions are needed.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;i\\\\-2i&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Both position and conjugation matter.</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-unitary", "number": "3.6", "title": "Unitary matrices and preserved geometry", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Verify unitarity by adjoint multiplication and column inner products.", "Explain preserved inner products and distinguish unitary, Hermitian and invertible."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/6.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-unitary-check-1", "question": "An invertible matrix preserves the norm of one chosen input. Is it necessarily unitary?", "options": ["Yes", "No; the condition must hold for all inputs.", "Yes, if that input is normalized."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["An invertible scaling can preserve one direction and stretch another.", "Correct. Verify U†U=I rather than one example.", "Normalization of one input does not establish a property of the full operator."]}, {"id": "math-unitary-check-2", "question": "Which statement is correct about a rank-one projector in a two-dimensional space?", "options": ["It is always a unitary gate.", "It is Hermitian and idempotent, but not invertible on the full space.", "It must preserve every vector’s norm."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["It annihilates a perpendicular component.", "Correct. These properties differ from unitarity.", "Projection generally shortens a vector."]}], "body": "<p class=\"lead\">A closed-system quantum gate must preserve inner products and hence normalization. Unitary matrices give us the algebraic condition that enforces this for every input, not merely for one example.</p>\n<section id=\"definition\"><h2>1. The unitary condition</h2><p>A square matrix U is unitary when</p>\n\\[U^\\dagger U=UU^\\dagger=I,\\qquad U^{-1}=U^\\dagger.\\]\n<p>Its columns form an orthonormal basis: the (j,k) entry of \\(U^\\dagger U\\) is the inner product of columns j and k. Consequently, checking all column norms and their mutual inner products checks unitarity. For a finite square matrix either identity condition implies the other.</p><h3>A complex worked example</h3>\n\\[U=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;i\\\\i&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad U^\\dagger=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-i\\\\-i&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n\\[U^\\dagger U=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1+(-i)i&amp;i-i\\\\-i+i&amp;(-i)i+1\\end{pmatrix}=I.\\]\n<p>Each diagonal becomes one after the factor 1/2, while the off-diagonal entries cancel. Applying U to basis state 0 gives \\((1,i)^{\\mathsf T}/\\sqrt2\\), whose squared norm is 1. The full matrix calculation establishes more than this one-input check: it establishes the condition for every vector.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/unitary.svg\" alt=\"A rotation preserves lengths and the angle between two real vectors, while unequal scaling changes them.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.6.</strong> Norm and inner-product preservation distinguish unitaries from arbitrary linear maps.</figcaption></figure>\n<div data-quiz=\"math-unitary-check-1\"></div></section><section id=\"preserve\"><h2>2. What is preserved?</h2>\n\\[\\langle Uu|Uv\\rangle=u^\\dagger U^\\dagger Uv=\\langle u|v\\rangle.\\]\n<p>Setting u=v proves norm preservation. Orthogonal vectors remain orthogonal. For real rotations this includes preserving ordinary angles and lengths; complex unitaries preserve the complex inner product. The two-dimensional rotation picture is an illustration, not a picture of all complex state spaces.</p><p>Consider the Hadamard matrix \\(H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}\\). It is real and symmetric, so \\(H^\\dagger=H\\). Multiplication gives \\(H^2=I\\). Thus H is both Hermitian and unitary. Many unitaries, such as \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\), are not Hermitian: \\(S^\\dagger=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-i)\\ne S\\).</p><div data-math-lab=\"unitary\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"scope\"><h2>3. Reversibility, composition and measurement</h2><p>If U and V are unitary, then \\((VU)^\\dagger(VU)=U^\\dagger V^\\dagger VU=I\\). Their composition is unitary, and its inverse applies the inverse operations in reverse order. This justifies describing an ideal circuit by a product of gates.</p><p>A projector such as diag(1,0) loses the second component and cannot be inverted on the whole space. It is not a unitary gate. Measurement and noisy evolution have their own descriptions; “all quantum operations are unitary” would be too broad. Unitarity describes the closed-system gate evolution considered here.</p><p>A further useful boundary is between unitary and merely invertible. The matrix diag(2,1) has an inverse, but it changes some lengths and is not unitary. Reversibility in linear algebra alone does not enforce quantum-state normalization.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-unitary-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Check a phase gate.</strong> Verify that \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) is unitary.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Conjugate the phase on the diagonal, then multiply.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Its adjoint is diag(1,−i); their product is diag(1,(−i)i)=I.</p></details></li><li><strong>Find the inverse order.</strong> A circuit applies H first, then S. Write its inverse.<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Reverse both the gates and their order.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The circuit matrix is SH. Its inverse is \\(H^\\dagger S^\\dagger=HS^\\dagger\\), so apply \\(S^\\dagger\\) first, then H.</p></details></li><li><strong>One input is not enough.</strong> Does diag(1,2) preserving the norm of basis state 0 prove unitarity?<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Test the other basis input or calculate the adjoint product.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. It doubles the norm of basis state 1. Its adjoint product is diag(1,4), so it fails the all-input condition.</p></details></li></ol></section>"}, {"id": "math-tensor", "number": "3.7", "title": "Tensor products and combined systems", "part": "Part A · Chapter 3", "chapter_id": "mathematical-language", "objectives": ["Expand vector and operator tensor products with explicit ordering.", "Distinguish inner, outer and tensor products by purpose and shape."], "prerequisites": "Chapter 2’s vectors, basis labels and matrix multiplication; elementary algebra. New notation is developed in the text.", "body_file": "chapters/mathematical-language/7.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "math-tensor-check-1", "question": "How many entries does the tensor product of a 3-entry and a 2-entry column have?", "options": ["5", "6", "1"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Five would be the concatenated length, not tensor dimension.", "Correct: 3×2=6 pairs of coordinates.", "A scalar is an inner-product output, and unequal dimensions would not support that inner product."]}, {"id": "math-tensor-check-2", "question": "Does exchanging two tensor factors always leave the displayed array unchanged?", "options": ["Yes", "No; the subsystem order determines the coordinate order.", "Yes, if both factors have unit norm."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Pairs of subsystem labels are ordered.", "Correct. A swap must be expressed explicitly.", "Normalization does not make tensor multiplication commutative."]}], "notebook": "04-mathematical-language.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "mathematics", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">A two-entry vector and another two-entry vector cannot describe a joint system by simply being added. To combine subsystem descriptions we need a product that keeps track of every pair of basis labels.</p>\n<section id=\"vectors\"><h2>1. Expand in a declared order</h2><p>For columns u=(a,b) and v=(c,d), define their tensor product by blocks:</p>\n\\[u\\otimes v=\\begin{pmatrix}av\\\\bv\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The coordinate order is (00,01,10,11), with the first label from u and the second from v. If u has m entries and v has n, the product has mn entries. This is not an inner product, outer product or concatenation. With u=(1,2) and v=(3,4), the answer is (3,4,6,8), rather than (1,2,3,4).</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/mathematical-language/tensor-expansion.svg\" alt=\"A two-by-two grid of coefficients ac, ad, bc, bd is read row by row into the joint vector.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.7.</strong> Tensor products enumerate pairs of labels in a specified order.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In ket notation, \\(|a\\rangle\\otimes|b\\rangle=|ab\\rangle\\). Bilinearity lets us expand:</p>\n\\[|+\\rangle\\otimes\\frac{|0\\rangle+i|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{|00\\rangle+i|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle+i|11\\rangle}{2}.\\]\n<p>Every pair of terms contributes its product of coefficients. The squared norm factors:</p>\n\\[\\|u\\otimes v\\|^2=\\sum_{j,k}|u_jv_k|^2=\\left(\\sum_j|u_j|^2\\right)\\left(\\sum_k|v_k|^2\\right).\\]\n<p>Thus normalized subsystem vectors give a normalized joint product vector. Not every normalized joint vector factors: the Bell-state example introduced in Chapter 1 is a counterexample. We will study separability and entanglement systematically in the composite-systems chapter.</p><div data-math-lab=\"tensor\"></div><div data-quiz=\"math-tensor-check-1\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"operators\"><h2>2. Tensor products of operators</h2><p>If A acts on the first subsystem and B on the second, their joint action satisfies</p>\n\\[(A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=(Au)\\otimes(Bv).\\]\n<p>In coordinates, each entry of A multiplies the whole block B. For a 2×2 A,</p>\n\\[A\\otimes B=\\begin{pmatrix}A_{00}B&amp;A_{01}B\\\\A_{10}B&amp;A_{11}B\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>For two qubits, \\(H\\otimes I\\) applies H to the first ket label, whereas \\(I\\otimes H\\) applies it to the second. In the declared order, \\((H\\otimes I)|01\\rangle=(|01\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). We transformed the first label, leaving the second equal to 1.</p><p>Swapping factors generally changes the coordinate array: u⊗v need not equal v⊗u. The systems can be related by an explicit swap, but silently exchanging them is a convention error. Programming libraries may display circuit wires and ket strings differently; declare the ordering before comparing a program with a hand calculation.</p><div data-quiz=\"math-tensor-check-2\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"comparison\"><h2>3. Three products, three questions</h2><table><caption>Keep the output type in view</caption><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Question</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inner: ⟨u|v⟩</td><td>What is their overlap?</td><td>Scalar (same vector dimension required)</td></tr><tr><td>Outer: |u⟩⟨v|</td><td>What rank-one map do they define?</td><td>Matrix with entries uⱼvₖ*</td></tr><tr><td>Tensor: u⊗v</td><td>How are separate spaces combined?</td><td>Vector with mn entries</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Identifying the type and shape before calculating is often the quickest error check. These operations all multiply entries, but their index rules and purposes differ.</p></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>Practice and explain</h2><p>Attempt each calculation before opening its solution. Keep intermediate steps and state the convention you use.</p><ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Expand.</strong> Compute \\((1,-1)^{\\mathsf T}\\otimes(1,i)^{\\mathsf T}/2\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Multiply every coefficient of the first factor by the entire second factor.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The entries are \\((1,i,-1,-i)^{\\mathsf T}/2\\). Their squared magnitudes sum to one.</p></details></li><li><strong>Track the label.</strong> In order (00,01,10,11), write \\(|1\\rangle\\otimes|0\\rangle\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Find the location of 10 in the declared order.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It is \\(|10\\rangle=(0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p></details></li><li><strong>Act on the second system.</strong> Find \\((I\\otimes H)|01\\rangle\\).<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Only the second ket label is changed.</p></details><details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>It is \\(|0\\rangle\\otimes H|1\\rangle=(|00\\rangle-|01\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\).</p></details></li></ol></section>"}], "legacyLessons": [{"id": "states", "title": "States, amplitudes & measurement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "time": "25 min", "objectives": ["Normalize a complex state vector.", "Distinguish amplitude from probability.", "Explain why phase can matter even when Z-basis probabilities do not change."], "body": "<h2>From probabilities to amplitudes</h2><p>A classical random bit is described by probabilities that sum to one. A pure qubit is described by two complex amplitudes: <strong>|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩</strong>. Their squared magnitudes sum to one: |α|² + |β|² = 1.</p><p>Computational-basis measurement returns 0 with probability |α|² and 1 with probability |β|². One measurement gives one classical outcome; it does not reveal both amplitudes.</p><h2>A worked example</h2><p>For |ψ⟩ = [|0⟩ + (1 + 3i)|1⟩]/√11, the squared magnitudes are 1/11 and (1² + 3²)/11 = 10/11. They add to one, so the state is normalized. This works through practice question Q1.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Keep this distinction</strong><p>Amplitudes can interfere. Probabilities are real, nonnegative numbers obtained after taking squared magnitudes.</p></aside><h2>Explore a pure qubit</h2><p>Up to global phase, |ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + exp(iφ) sin(θ/2)|1⟩. The Bloch coordinates are (sin θ cos φ, sin θ sin φ, cos θ). Change φ while holding θ fixed. Predict which measurement probabilities will change.</p>", "lab": "bloch", "quiz": {"question": "With θ = π/2, changing φ from 0 to π changes which quantity?", "options": ["The Z-basis probability of 1", "The X-basis probability of +", "The normalization"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Z-basis probabilities remain 1/2. The state changes from |+⟩ to |−⟩, so the X-basis probability of + changes from 1 to 0."}, "exercise": "Solve Q2–5 and Q10 in your practice sheet. For Q2, use |1−i|² = 2 before normalizing.", "solution": "Q2: N = 1/√6 and vector (1−i, 2)ᵀ/√6. Q10: θ=π/2, φ=π gives |−⟩.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "gates", "title": "Gates, phase & interference", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "time": "25 min", "objectives": ["Apply gates in circuit order.", "Convert relative phase into measurement statistics.", "Recognize global-phase equivalence."], "body": "<h2>Operations preserve normalization</h2><p>A closed-system quantum gate is unitary: U†U = I. The Hadamard gate maps |0⟩ to |+⟩ and |1⟩ to |−⟩. The Z gate leaves |0⟩ unchanged and negates the amplitude of |1⟩.</p><h2>Work through H → Z → H</h2><p>Start in |0⟩. The first H prepares (|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2. Z changes this to (|0⟩−|1⟩)/√2. The final H gives |1⟩. The phase difference becomes a deterministic measurement outcome. Matrix multiplication writes the same sequence as HZH|0⟩, with the rightmost operator acting first.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Same probabilities, different states</strong><p>|+⟩ and |−⟩ have identical computational-basis measurement probabilities. Applying H before measurement distinguishes them.</p></aside><h2>Use the existing gate lab</h2><p>Try Pauli Z on |+⟩, then compare with Z on |0⟩. Predict the end point before playing each rotation. The supplied animation is retained as a separate lab.</p><p><a href=\"animations/pauli_gate_bloch_sphere_animation.html\">Open Pauli gate / Bloch sphere lab ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "What is the result of H → Z → H applied to |0⟩?", "options": ["|0⟩", "|1⟩", "A 50/50 Z-basis distribution"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "H prepares |+⟩, Z turns it into |−⟩, and H maps |−⟩ to |1⟩."}, "exercise": "Solve Q6–9. Compare XZ|ψ⟩ and ZX|ψ⟩: do their different signs imply distinguishable physical states?", "solution": "XZ = −ZX. For any input the outputs differ by global phase −1, so they give identical statistics for all measurements.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "entanglement", "title": "Two qubits & entanglement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "time": "30 min", "objectives": ["Expand tensor products in a fixed basis order.", "Test pure two-qubit states for separability.", "Prepare and measure a Bell state in Qiskit."], "body": "<h2>Two systems, one state</h2><p>We use basis order |00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩. A two-qubit pure state is a|00⟩ + b|01⟩ + c|10⟩ + d|11⟩. Product states factor into two single-qubit vectors. For a nonzero normalized pure state, factorization is possible exactly when ad − bc = 0.</p><h2>Build a Bell state</h2><p>Begin at |00⟩. Apply H to the left qubit, giving (|00⟩+|10⟩)/√2. A CNOT controlled by that qubit gives (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. Here ad−bc=1/2, so the state is entangled.</p><p>Each individual bit is random in computational-basis measurement, but their outcomes always agree. Correlations in one measurement basis alone are not proof of entanglement: a classical mixture of 00 and 11 has those correlations too.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Watch the wire order</strong><p>Qiskit displays q₀ at the top, but a two-qubit ket is written |q₁q₀⟩. To implement the left-qubit Bell construction above, use h(1), then cx(1, 0).</p></aside><h2>Partial measurement</h2><p>Project onto the observed outcome and divide surviving amplitudes by the square root of its probability. For a Bell state, observing left bit 0 leaves |00⟩ and observing 1 leaves |11⟩. A zero-probability outcome has no conditional state.</p><p><a href=\"animations/quantum_computing_interactive_companion.html\">Open tensor, measurement & Bell-state companion ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "For a normalized pure state with coefficients a,b,c,d, when is it separable?", "options": ["a+b+c+d = 1", "ad−bc = 0", "All four probabilities are equal"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The 2×2 coefficient matrix has rank one exactly when its determinant ad−bc vanishes. This criterion applies to pure two-qubit states."}, "exercise": "Solve Q15, Q16, Q20 and Q25. Then compare Bell-state measurements in Z and X bases in the notebook.", "solution": "Q16: P(left=0)=1/2 and P(left=1)=1/2. Conditional full states are (|00⟩+|01⟩)/√2 and |11⟩. Q25: q₀=1, q₁=0 means |01⟩.", "notebook": "02-bell-states.ipynb"}, {"id": "classifier", "title": "A first variational classifier", "part": "04 · Learning with circuits", "time": "35 min", "objectives": ["Separate data encoding from trainable parameters.", "Read a probability as a model prediction.", "Compute an expectation gradient by parameter shift."], "body": "<h2>A quantum model inside a classical loop</h2><p>Encode a scalar feature x with Rᵧ(x), apply a trainable Rᵧ(w), then measure. Starting from |0⟩, the probability of label 1 is p(1|x,w) = sin²((x+w)/2). A classical optimizer updates w to reduce a loss over training examples.</p><p>This tiny model combines the two rotations into one. It is an intentionally limited teaching model that a classical computer simulates easily. It demonstrates the training loop and makes no claim of quantum advantage.</p><h2>From probability to expectation</h2><p>The Z expectation is f(x,w)=1−2p(1|x,w)=cos(x+w). For this rotation, ∂f/∂w = [f(x,w+π/2)−f(x,w−π/2)]/2. A loss gradient still needs the chain rule; shifting the whole loss is not generally the same calculation.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Evaluate the complete experiment</strong><p>Fit preprocessing on training data only. Keep a test set for final evaluation. Compare classical baselines and report circuit depth, sampling cost, seeds, and uncertainty.</p></aside><h2>Explore before training</h2><p>Move x and w to predict the circuit output. The notebook implements Qiskit statevector predictions, parameter-shift gradients, a small training loop, and a held-out comparison.</p>", "lab": "classifier", "quiz": {"question": "For x=0 and w=π, what is p(1)?", "options": ["0", "1/2", "1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Rᵧ(π)|0⟩ = |1⟩, so sin²(π/2)=1."}, "exercise": "Derive ∂p(1|x,w)/∂w. Explain why changing the number of shots changes uncertainty but not the ideal probability.", "solution": "∂p/∂w = sin(x+w)/2. For N independent shots the estimated probability has variance p(1−p)/N.", "notebook": "03-variational-classifier.ipynb"}], "planned": [{"title": "Part A · Chapter 4 · Qubits and measurement", "needs": "Pure states, Born probabilities, phase, and conditional projection."}, {"title": "Part A · Chapter 5 · Gates, interference and measurement bases", "needs": "Unitary evolution, gates, rotations, and Bloch geometry."}, {"title": "Part A · Chapter 6 · Composite systems and entanglement", "needs": "Tensor products, controlled gates, Bell states, and partial measurement."}, {"title": "Part A · Chapter 7 · Registers, conventions and oracles", "needs": "Circuit/readout ordering and reversible oracle access."}, {"title": "Part A · Chapter 8 · Learning from quantum algorithms", "needs": "Fully worked kickback and Deutsch; a promised Deutsch–Jozsa example. Simon is an optional extension."}, {"title": "Part A · Chapter 9 · Observables, statistics and noisy devices", "needs": "Readout and finite-shot estimates before QML use, with linked depth on mixtures, reduced states and noise."}, {"title": "Part B · Classical machine learning bridge", "needs": "Learning loops, optimization, feature spaces, kernels, and generalization."}, {"title": "Part C · Quantum machine learning", "needs": "Encoding, variational classifiers, quantum kernels, and responsible evaluation."}]};
